{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794544718942,"sessionID":"ses_e2ccd29cbffeOwNLR1Qo8Fv8oA","part":{"partID":"prt_1d33351fe001zzopP1bC6TZYPZ","sessionID":"ses_e2ccd29cbffeOwNLR1Qo8Fv8oA","messageID":"msg_1d3333af9001OlcB5AR433xviP","type":"tool","id":"call_aa2292ef5f1e4226a803ac9a","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/012-plain-offer-reach/tasks.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/012-plain-offer-reach/tasks.md, lines 1-303\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Tasks: Phase 12: plain-offer-reach\"\n3: description: \"Ordered worker-sized tasks in five waves: name the matched doctor command in the hand-off, add sk-doc paraphrase aliases, label a hub that needs a mode, pair each deep mode with a scorer anchor and a hub alias and, only when the floors stay put, anchor three create rows. Each wave ends with a recapture and a floor check.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"plain offer reach tasks\"\n6: importance_tier: \"normal\"\n7: contextType: \"general\"\n8: ---\n9: <!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: tasks-core | v2.4 -->\n10: # Tasks: Phase 12: plain-offer-reach\n11: \n12: <!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 2 -->\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: <!-- ANCHOR:notation -->\n17: ## Task Notation\n18: \n19: | Prefix | Meaning |\n20: |--------|---------|\n21: | `[ ]` | Pending |\n22: | `[x]` | Completed |\n23: | `[P]` | Parallelizable |\n24: | `[B]` | Blocked |\n25: | `[C]` | Coordinator task: a decision, a revert, a live hook run or an operator step. No worker runs it |\n26: \n27: **Task Format**: `T### [P?] Description (file path). Verify: <command> prints <expected output>`\n28: \n29: Every command runs from the repository root unless a task says otherwise.\n30: - `ADV` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime`. `TC` is `npm run typecheck` in `ADV`, which must exit 0. `BLD` is `npm run build` in `ADV`, which must exit 0. `SP` is this phase's `scratch/` folder.\n31: - `DH` is `$ADV/lib/scorer/doctor-helper-handoff.ts` and `DHT` its test `$ADV/tests/doctor-helper-handoff.vitest.ts`. `VDHT` is `npx vitest run tests/doctor-helper-handoff.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n32: - `FU` is `$ADV/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`. `FHT` is `$ADV/tests/scorer/doctor-handoff-command.vitest.ts` (new) and `VFHT` is `npx vitest run tests/scorer/doctor-handoff-command.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n33: - `DP` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/doctor-prompts.jsonl`. `VDS` is `npx vitest run tests/doctor-routing-slice.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n34: - `CA` is `$ADV/lib/command-attach.ts` and `CAT` its test `$ADV/tests/command-attach.vitest.ts`. `VCA` is `npx vitest run tests/command-attach.vitest.ts` in `ADV`. `DBC` is `node $ADV/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs --check | jq -r .status`, which must print `fresh`.\n35: - `SCH` is `$ADV/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` and `VSCH` is `npx vitest run tests/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.vitest.ts` in `ADV`. `SUB` is `$ADV/lib/subprocess.ts`.\n36: - `AR` is `$ADV/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` and `ARC` its test `$ADV/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts`. `VARC` is `npx vitest run tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n37: - `HFB` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts` and `HFT` its test `$ADV/tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-envelope.vitest.ts`. `VHFT` is `npx vitest run tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-envelope.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n38: - `RD` is `$ADV/lib/render.ts` and `GT` is `$ADV/tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts`. `VGT` is `npx vitest run tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts tests/command-head-length.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n39: - `EX` is `$ADV/lib/scorer/lanes/explicit.ts`. `VEX` is `npx vitest run tests/command-bridge-resolution-guard.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n40: - `HRD` is `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/hub-router.json` and `HRL` is `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/hub-router.json`. `CF_DOC` is `.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/009-parent-hub-rollout/007-sk-doc/fixtures/canary-cases.v1.json` and `CF_LOOP` is `.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/009-parent-hub-rollout/002-system-deep-loop/fixtures/canary-cases.v1.json`.\n41: - `MS` is `.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation` (the served manifests) and `MA` is `specs/sk-doc/z_archive/019-skill-routing-refactor/015-router-unification-program/013-live-activation/activation` (their authored twins).\n42: - `REFRESH <hub>` is three commands in order: `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-manifest.cjs refresh --hub <hub> --skill-root .skilled/skills/<hub>`, then `cp $MS/<hub>/manifest.json $MA/<hub>/manifest.json`, then `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-guard.cjs --json | jq .failures`, which must print `0`.\n43: - `HUBOK <hub>` is four checks: `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-manifest.cjs freshness --hub <hub> --skill-root .skilled/skills/<hub> | jq -r .causeCode` prints `fresh`, `cmp $MS/<hub>/manifest.json $MA/<hub>/manifest.json` prints nothing, `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-sync.cjs --check | tail -1` prints `all 55 served copies match their authored files` and `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs .skilled/skills/<hub> | tail -1` prints a line that starts with `OK: parent-skill-check`.\n44: - `BINT` is `node --test .skilled/bin/tests/compiled-route-*.test.cjs`, which must print `ℹ fail 0`.\n45: - `CAP` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs`. `BASE` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-routing-baseline.json`. `CORPUS` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-prompts.jsonl`. `RS` is `npx vitest run tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n46: - `RT` is a shell function that prints the compiled route of every non-holdout corpus row and every canary prompt on one hub, as `<id><TAB>{\"action\":...,\"t\":[...]}`, and never prints a prompt. Define it in the shell first: `RT() { { jq -r 'select(.split!=\"holdout\") | [.id, .prompt] | @tsv' \"$CORPUS\"; jq -r '.cases[] | select(.prompt) | [\"canary:\" + .id, .prompt] | @tsv' \"$2\"; } | while IFS=$'\\t' read -r id p; do printf '%s\\t%s\\n' \"$id\" \"$(printf '%s' \"$p\" | node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub \"$1\" --prompt-stdin | jq -c '{action, t: [.targets[]? | .workflowMode]}')\"; done; }`. Call it as `RT sk-doc $CF_DOC` (154 lines) or `RT system-deep-loop $CF_LOOP` (139 lines).\n47: - `FLOOR` is `node $CAP | jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer'`, which must print at least `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and exactly `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`.\n48: - `TYPED` is `node $CAP | jq -c '.attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached, .attachedMetrics.typed'`, which must print exactly `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` and the typed line in `$SP/before.txt` (`{\"own\":24,\"attached\":20,\"combined\":44,\"total\":44}` once phase 011 is committed).\n49: - `PLAIN` is `node $CAP | jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain.tune, .attachedMetrics.plain.holdout'`. It is a measurement, not a gate. The holdout line is a count and never a prompt.\n50: - `EVAL` is `node $ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs | jq -c '[.metrics.full_corpus_top1.correct,.metrics.holdout_top1.correct,.metrics.ambiguity_top1.correct,.metrics.gold_none_false_fire.value]'`, which must print numbers no lower than `[159,50,20]` and no lower than its line in `$SP/before.txt`, with the fourth no higher than 5.\n51: - `PY` is `python3 $ADV/tests/python/test_skill_advisor.py`, which must exit 0. `PAR` is `npx vitest run tests/parity/python-ts-parity.vitest.ts` in `ADV`, which must report 0 failed. `SUITE` is `npx vitest run` in `ADV`, whose `Test Files` and `Tests` summary lines must name no `failed` count.\n52: - `FLOORCHECK <X>` is: run `BLD`, then `FLOOR`, `TYPED`, `PLAIN`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `PY`, `PAR`, `RS`, `VDS`, `BINT`, `GUARD` (`node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-guard.cjs --json | jq .failures`) and `SUITE`, and write into `$SP/after-<X>.txt` one line per output line, each prefixed with the shorthand name and a colon, such as `FLOOR: {\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` or `TC: exit 0`. For the test runners write the summary line. It passes when every gate above prints what it must. `$SP/before.txt` uses the same format.\n53: \n54: Rules for every task:\n55: - Each task is one change to the files it names. A task that names a module and its test adds the behaviour and the cases that pin it.\n56: - No task reads or prints holdout prompt text. A script filters on `split` before it uses a prompt and prints row ids only. Tune and none rows may be read.\n57: - No code comment may name a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id. State the durable reason instead. T032 checks it.\n58: - No task writes to the home folder, installs anything, adds a dependency, edits `AGENTS.md`, edits a file under `.skilled/commands/create/`, edits a `ROUTER.md` or edits a canary fixture.\n59: - A task that edits a hub `hub-router.json` ends with `REFRESH <hub>` and `HUBOK <hub>` in the same task.\n60: - The brief head keeps the phase 005 form. The `(needs a mode)` label is the one addition, and `GT` is re-pinned in the same task as the renderer.\n61: - A `.md` edit routes through `sk-doc` and a code edit through `sk-code` before the first write. Every task re-reads its anchor text first, because phase 011 edited the same files.\n62: \n63: Worker waves. Tasks in one wave touch different files and can run in parallel. Each task owns the files in parentheses. A wave starts only after the previous wave's last task printed its expected output.\n64: - Wave 0: T001 (`$SP/before.txt`), T002 (`$SP/routes-*-before.tsv`), T003 (`$SP/plain-before.json`). Read-only for the repository.\n65: - Gate: no task from wave A1 on starts before the coordinator confirms that phase 011 is committed, because it edits `CA`, `FU`, `AR`, `SCH`, `CAP` and `BASE`.\n66: - Wave A1: T004 (`DH`, `DHT`).\n67: - Wave A2: T005 (`FU`, `FHT`).\n68: - Wave A3: T006 (`DP`), T007 (the doctor feature catalog page).\n69: - Wave A4: T008 (`BASE`). Wave A5: T009 (`$SP/after-A.txt`).\n70: - Wave B1: T010 (`HRD`, both `sk-doc` manifests). Wave B2: T011 (`BASE`). Wave B3: T012 (`$SP/after-B.txt`).\n71: - Wave C1: T013 (`CA`, `CAT`), T014 (`SCH`), T015 (`SUB`).\n72: - Wave C2: T016 (`AR`, `ARC`), T017 (`RD`, `GT`).\n73: - Wave C3: T018 (`HFB`, `HFT`), T019 (the hook guide). Wave C4: T020 (`$SP/after-C.txt`).\n74: - Wave D1: T021 (`EX`), T022 (`HRL`, both deep-loop manifests). Wave D2: T023 (`BASE`). Wave D3: T024 (`$SP/after-D.txt`). Wave D4: T025 [C].\n75: - Wave E1: T026 (`EX`). Wave E2: T027 (`BASE`). Wave E3: T028 (`$SP/after-E.txt`). Wave E4: T029 [C].\n76: - Verification, one at a time: T030, T031, T032, T033 [C], T034.\n77: \n78: Expected plain tune line (`.attachedMetrics.plain.tune`) per wave. Only the floors are a hard gate:\n79: \n80: | After | own | attached | combined |\n81: |-------|-----|----------|----------|\n82: | Wave 0 | 2 | 7 | 9 |\n83: | A | 7 | 7 | 14 |\n84: | B | 7 | 10 | 17 |\n85: | C | 7 | 10 | 17 |\n86: | D | 7 | 13 | 20 |\n87: | E | 8 | 15 | 23 |\n88: <!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n89: \n90: ---\n91: \n92: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n93: ## Phase 1: Setup\n94: \n95: - [x] T001 Record the before numbers (`$SP/before.txt`). Run `BLD` only if `node $CAP` refuses a stale build, then run `FLOOR`, `TYPED`, `PLAIN`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `PY`, `PAR`, `RS`, `VDS`, `VCA`, `VGT`, `VDHT`, `VARC`, `VHFT`, `VSCH`, `VEX`, `DBC`, `BINT`, `SUITE`, `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-guard.cjs --json | jq .failures`, `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-sync.cjs --check | tail -1` and `HUBOK sk-doc` and `HUBOK system-deep-loop`, and write them into `before.txt` in the `FLOORCHECK` line format. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `TYPED` prints `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` and a typed line, `PLAIN` prints `{\"own\":2,\"attached\":7,\"combined\":9,\"total\":45}` first, `EVAL` prints numbers no lower than `[159,50,20]` with a fourth no higher than 5, `TC` and `PY` exit 0, the guard prints `0` and `wc -l < $SP/before.txt` prints at least 25. When a gate is red here, stop and mark the phase `[B]` with that line, because a red before number cannot be held. When `SUITE` names failed files, list them in `before.txt`, and from then on the `SUITE` gate is \"no failed file outside that list\".\n96: - [x] T002 Record every compiled route before any hub edit (`$SP/routes-sk-doc-before.tsv`, `$SP/routes-deep-loop-before.tsv`). Define `RT` and run `RT sk-doc $CF_DOC > $SP/routes-sk-doc-before.tsv` and `RT system-deep-loop $CF_LOOP > $SP/routes-deep-loop-before.tsv`. Verify: `wc -l < $SP/routes-sk-doc-before.tsv` prints 154, `wc -l < $SP/routes-deep-loop-before.tsv` prints 139, `rg -c 'holdout' $SP/routes-*-before.tsv` prints nothing or 0 for both and `rg -n '^nm-system-deep-loop-01' $SP/routes-deep-loop-before.tsv` prints `nm-system-deep-loop-01\t{\"action\":\"route\",\"t\":[\"research\"]}`.\n97: - [x] T003 Record each plain tune row's state (`$SP/plain-before.json`): run `node $CAP | jq '.attachedMetrics.attachedRows as $a | [.rows | to_entries[] | select(.key | test(\"plain-tune\")) | {id: .key, expected: .value.expected_command, top: .value.top, top1: .value.top1, top3: .value.top3, attached: ($a[.key] // null)}]' > $SP/plain-before.json`. Verify: `jq 'length' $SP/plain-before.json` prints 45, `jq '[.[] | select(.top1)] | length' $SP/plain-before.json` prints 2 and `jq -r '.[] | select(.id==\"cmd-doctor-ci-plain-tune-01\") | .top' $SP/plain-before.json` prints `command-doctor-helper`.\n98: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n99: \n100: ---\n101: \n102: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n103: ## Phase 2: Implementation\n104: \n105: ### Wave A1: the doctor hand-off module\n106: - [x] T004 Return the ranked runnable, add the gerund cue and add a repair request cue (`DH`, `DHT`). In `DH`: (1) in `interface HelperCandidate` add `readonly runnable?: string;` after `readonly command: string;`. (2) In `interface DoctorProblemMatch` add `readonly runnable: string;` after `readonly command: string;`, with the doc comment `` /** The command with its subcommand, such as `/doctor:agents-md check`. */ ``. (3) In `matchDoctorProblem`, change the returned object to `{ command: first.command, runnable: typeof first.runnable === 'string' && first.runnable !== '' ? first.runnable : first.command, score: first.score }`. (4) In `STRONG_PROBLEM_CUE`, add the line `  '\\\\bstopped converg\\\\w*\\\\b',` right after the line that starts `  '\\\\bnever (?:converg`. (5) After the `WEAK_PROBLEM_CUE` declaration add `const GENERIC_REPAIR_CUE = /\\b(?:repair path|i have a problem)\\b/i;` with the comment: a request for a repair route that names no problem the ranker knows still belongs to /doctor:helper, which lists the routes and their reasons, and the two phrases are exact so ordinary work requests never carry them. (6) After `doctorProblemCue` add `export function genericRepairCue(prompt: string): boolean { return GENERIC_REPAIR_CUE.test(prompt); }` with the doc comment `/** True when the prompt asks for a repair route without naming the problem. */`. (7) In the module header, replace `and if it does the advisor offers` and the line after it, `/doctor:helper, which then names the command.`, with `and if it does the advisor offers the command the ranker named, or` and `/doctor:helper when that command asks before it runs.`. In `DHT` add cases: `doctorProblemCue('my research loop stopped converging and I cannot see why')` is `'strong'`, `matchDoctorProblem('confirm the AGENTS.md sections in this repo have not drifted', WORKSPACE)?.runnable` is `'/doctor:agents-md check'`, `matchDoctorProblem('show me every GitHub Actions workflow and whether each is enabled', WORKSPACE)?.runn... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n107: \n108: ### Wave A2: the hand-off inserts the named command\n109: - [x] T005 Insert the ranked `run` command instead of the helper (`FU`, `FHT`). In `FU`: (1) add `COMMAND_ROWS` to the import from `'../command-attach.js'` and `genericRepairCue` to the import from `'./doctor-helper-handoff.js'`. (2) Right above the comment block that precedes `export function applyDoctorHelperHandoff(`, add `export function runnableDoctorBridgeId(runnable: string, projection: AdvisorProjection): string | undefined` with this body: for each `[id, row]` of `Object.entries(COMMAND_ROWS)`, skip it unless `id.startsWith(DOCTOR_BRIDGE_PREFIX)`, `row.command === runnable` and `row.policy === 'run'`, and return `id` when `projection.skills.some((skill) => skill.id === id)`. After the loop return `undefined`. Its comment says the ranker names a runnable such as `/doctor:agents-md check`, and this returns that bridge when it runs without asking and the projection holds it. (3) Replace the four comment lines above `applyDoctorHelperHandoff` with: `// A doctor problem described in other words than a bridge's own phrases reaches`, `// no doctor bridge. When none passes and none was typed, /doctor:helper's ranker`, `// decides whether the prompt is a doctor problem. If it is, the command it ranks`, `// first passes when that command runs without asking, and the helper passes`, `// otherwise. A plain request for a repair route with no ranked command and no`, `// passing skill also gets the helper.`. (4) In the function, replace `if (!match) return [...ranked];` with `if (!match && (skillPasses || !genericRepairCue(context.prompt))) return [...ranked];`, and prefix the next condition with `match && ` so it reads `if (match && cue === null && match.score < DOCTOR_HELPER_UNCUED_MIN_SCORE) return [...ranked];`. (5) Before `const existing =` add `const handoffId = (match ? runnableDoctorBridgeId(match.runnable, context.projection) : undefined) ?? DOCTOR_HELPER_BRIDGE_ID;` and `const handoffSkill = context.projection.skills.find((skill) => skill.id === handoffId) ?? he... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n110: \n111: ### Wave A3\n112: - [x] T006 [P] Relabel the three hand-off rows that now get a named command (`DP`). Change exactly three lines and nothing else. In the line with `\"id\":\"doctor-b-helper-handoff-01\"`, replace `\"expected_top\":\"command-doctor-helper\"` with `\"expected_top\":\"command-doctor-agents-md-check\"`. In the line with `\"id\":\"doctor-b-helper-handoff-02\"`, replace `\"expected_second\":\"command-doctor-helper\"` with `\"expected_second\":\"command-doctor-deep-loop\"`. In the line with `\"id\":\"doctor-b-helper-handoff-04\"`, replace `\"expected_top\":\"command-doctor-helper\"` with `\"expected_top\":\"command-doctor-setup\"`. Verify: `VDS` reports 0 failed, `rg -c '\"expected_top\":\"command-doctor-helper\"' $DP` prints 3, `rg -c '\"expected_second\":\"command-doctor-helper\"' $DP` prints 1 and `wc -l < $DP` prints 78.\n113: - [x] T007 [P] In `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md`, in the paragraph that starts `A doctor bridge passes on its own wording`, replace the text `` and if it does the `/doctor:helper` bridge passes so the operator is offered the command that names the right one. `` with the text `` and if it does the command the ranker puts first passes by name when its policy is `run`, and `/doctor:helper` passes instead when that command asks first or has no bridge. A prompt that asks for a `repair path` or says `I have a problem`, with no ranked command and no passing skill, gets `/doctor:helper`. `stopped converging` counts as a wrong state beside a passing skill. `` (both without the outer spaces). Verify: `rg -c \"repair path\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md` prints 1 and `rg -c \"is offered the command that names the right one\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md` prints nothing or 0.\n114: \n115: ### Wave A4\n116: - [x] T008 Rebuild and recapture after wave A (`BASE`). Run `BLD`, then `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain.tune' $BASE` prints `{\"own\":7,\"attached\":7,\"combined\":14,\"total\":45}` (expectation), `jq -r '.rows[\"cmd-doctor-agents-md-plain-tune-01\"].top, .rows[\"cmd-doctor-ci-plain-tune-01\"].top, .rows[\"cmd-doctor-helper-plain-tune-01\"].top, .rows[\"cmd-doctor-deep-loop-plain-tune-01\"].top3' $BASE` prints `command-doctor-agents-md-check`, `command-doctor-ci-report`, `command-doctor-helper` and `true`, `jq -c '.metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached' $BASE` prints `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` twice and `RS` reports 0 failed. When the tune line differs from the expectation but the floors hold, write the actual line into `$SP/after-A.txt` in T009 and continue.\n117: \n118: ### Wave A5\n119: - [x] T009 Floor check after wave A (`$SP/after-A.txt`). Run `FLOORCHECK A`. Verify: every gate in `FLOORCHECK` prints what it must, and `diff <(rg '^(TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/before.txt) <(rg '^(TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-A.txt)` prints nothing, because the simulation moved no typed or scorer eval row. When a gate fails or that diff prints a line, mark T010 `[B]` with that line and stop.\n120: \n121: ### Wave B1: sk-doc paraphrase aliases\n122: - [ ] T010 Add six task-shaped aliases to the `sk-doc` hub and refresh it (`HRD`, `$MS/sk-doc/manifest.json`, `$MA/sk-doc/manifest.json`). In `HRD` `vocabularyClasses`, append to the end of each `keywords` array: `create-agent-aliases` gets `\"agent definition\"` and `\"specialist persona file\"`, `create-diff-aliases` gets `\"before and after my rewrite\"`, `create-goal-aliases` gets `\"intent file\"` and `create-command-aliases` gets `\"prompt shortcut\"` and `\"confirm flag\"`. Change nothing else, and keep the file's two-space indentation. Then run `REFRESH sk-doc`, `HUBOK sk-doc`, `RT sk-doc $CF_DOC > $SP/routes-sk-doc-after.tsv` and `BINT`. Verify: `jq '[.vocabularyClasses[].keywords | length] | add' $HRD` prints 6 more than before the edit, `diff $SP/routes-sk-doc-before.tsv $SP/routes-sk-doc-after.tsv | rg '^>' | cut -f1 | tr '\\n' ' '` prints `> cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-01 > cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-02 > cmd-create-command-plain-tune-01 > cmd-create-diff-plain-tune-01 > cmd-create-goal-plain-tune-01 `, `rg -n 'sk-create-(agent|command|diff|goal)' $SP/routes-sk-doc-after.tsv | rg -c 'plain-tune'` prints at least 5, `REFRESH` printed `0`, every `HUBOK` check prints what it must and `BINT` prints `ℹ fail 0`. When the diff lists any other id, in particular an `mns-`, `nm-`, `sv-` or `canary:` id, revert `HRD`, run `REFRESH sk-doc` again and mark the task `[B]` with that id.\n123: \n124: ### Wave B2\n125: - [ ] T011 Rebuild and recapture after wave B (`BASE`). Run `BLD`, then `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain.tune' $BASE` prints `{\"own\":7,\"attached\":10,\"combined\":17,\"total\":45}` (expectation), `jq -r '.attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-01\"], .attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-02\"], .attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-create-diff-plain-tune-01\"]' $BASE` prints `/create:agent`, `/create:agent` and `/create:diff`, `jq -c '.metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached' $BASE` prints `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` twice and `RS` reports 0 failed.\n126: \n127: ### Wave B3\n128: - [ ] T012 Floor check after wave B (`$SP/after-B.txt`). Run `FLOORCHECK B`. Verify: every gate in `FLOORCHECK` prints what it must. When a gate fails, mark T013 `[B]` with that line and stop.\n129: \n130: ### Wave C1: the mode flag\n131: - [ ] T013 [P] Add `routeNeedsMode` (`CA`, `CAT`). At the end of `CA`, after `attachCommand`, add `export function routeNeedsMode(skillId: string, compiledRoute: unknown): boolean` whose body is: `const route = asRecord(compiledRoute);`, return false when `!route`, return false when `route.action !== 'defer' && route.action !== 'clarify'`, and return `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(MODE_COMMANDS, skillId)`. Its comment says a hub that withholds its command because the prompt names no single mode must still tell the reader why no command came with it, and only a hub whose modes map to commands withholds one. Do not touch the generated blocks. In `CAT` add cases: `routeNeedsMode('system-deep-loop', { action: 'defer' })` is true, `routeNeedsMode('sk-doc', { action: 'clarify' })` is true, `routeNeedsMode('sk-doc', { action: 'route', selectionKind: 'single', targets: [{ workflowMode: 'sk-create-readme' }] })` is false, `routeNeedsMode('sk-code', { action: 'defer' })` is false, `routeNeedsMode('sk-doc', undefined)` is false and `routeNeedsMode('sk-doc', ['defer'])` is false. Verify: `VCA` reports 0 failed, `TC` exits 0 and `DBC` prints `fresh`.\n132: - [ ] T014 [P] Add `needsMode` to the recommendation schema (`SCH`). In `AdvisorRecommendationSchema`, right after `ownerSkillId: z.string().min(1).optional(),`, add `needsMode: z.literal(true).optional(),` with the comment: set only when a hub held back its command because the prompt names no single mode, so the head can say so. Verify: `rg -c \"needsMode\" $SCH` prints 1, `VSCH` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n133: - [ ] T015 [P] Add `needsMode` to `AdvisorRecommendation` (`SUB`). In `export interface AdvisorRecommendation`, right after `readonly ownerSkillId?: string;`, add `readonly needsMode?: boolean;` with the comment: true when a hub held back its command because the prompt names no single mode. Verify: `rg -c \"readonly needsMode\" $SUB` prints 1 and `TC` exits 0.\n134: \n135: ### Wave C2\n136: - [ ] T016 [P] Set `needsMode` in the compiled route enrichment (`AR`, `ARC`). Add `routeNeedsMode` to the import from `'../lib/command-attach.js'`. In `enrichCompiledRoutes`, replace the last branch of the return, `: { ...recommendation, compiledRoute };`, with `: { ...recommendation, compiledRoute, ...(!command && routeNeedsMode(recommendation.skillId, compiledRoute) ? { needsMode: true as const } : {}) };`. In `ARC`, add `needsMode?: boolean` to the recommendation type of `ResponsePayload` and add one case: mock `execFileSync` to return `JSON.stringify({ action: 'defer', hubId: 'sk-doc', selectionKind: null, targets: [] })`, call `handleAdvisorRecommend` with prompt `Write a README for the ledger module` and `options: { includeCompiledRoute: true }`, and expect the `sk-doc` row to have `needsMode` true and no `command`. In the same case, mock a `defer` for `sk-code` and call it with prompt `Implement a TypeScript feature with focused tests for the ledger.`, and expect the `sk-code` row to have no `needsMode` key. Use prompts no other case in the file uses, because the handler caches by prompt. Verify: `VARC` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n137: - [ ] T017 [P] Print `(needs a mode)` and re-pin the golden heads (`RD`, `GT`). In `renderAdvisorBrief`, right after the `if (!topLabel) { ... }` block, add `` const headLabel = top.needsMode === true && top.command === undefined && commandRowFor(top.skill) === undefined ? `${topLabel} (needs a mode)` : topLabel; `` with the comment: a hub that withheld its command because the prompt names no single mode says so, so the reader asks for the mode instead of improvising one. In both head strings, the ambiguous one and the `use` one, replace `${topLabel}` with `${headLabel}`. Change nothing else in `RD`. In `GT`, add `readonly needsMode?: boolean;` to `RecommendationInput` and add two `HEAD_CASES`: name `a hub that needs a mode says so instead of naming a command` with `{ skill: 'system-deep-loop', needsMode: true, confidence: 0.82, uncertainty: 0.2 }` and `expectedHead` `Advisor: live; use system-deep-loop (needs a mode) 0.82/0.20 pass.`, and name `a command on the hub wins over the mode flag` with `{ skill: 'system-deep-loop', kind: 'command', command: '/deep:review', policy: 'ask', ownerSkillId: 'system-deep-loop', needsMode: true, confidence: 0.82, uncertainty: 0.16 }` and `expectedHead` `Advisor: live; use system-deep-loop → /deep:review (ask) 0.82/0.16 pass.`. Every existing case keeps its `expectedHead`. Verify: `VGT` reports 0 failed, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -- $GT | rg -c '^-.*expectedHead'` prints nothing or 0 and `TC` exits 0.\n138: \n139: ### Wave C3\n140: - [ ] T018 [P] Keep `needsMode` from the CLI (`HFB`, `HFT`). In `recommendationFromCli`, right after the spread that keeps `ownerSkillId`, add `...(value.needsMode === true ? { needsMode: true } : {}),` with the comment: a hub that held back its command for want of a mode keeps that signal, so the head can say why no command came with it. In `HFT` add one case in the shape of `renders a CLI row without command fields from the generated table`: `resultFromCliData` with `recommendations: [{ skillId: 'system-deep-loop', confidence: 0.89, uncertainty: 0.12, needsMode: true }]`, and expect the first line of `renderAdvisorBrief(result)` to contain `use system-deep-loop (needs a mode)`. Verify: `VHFT` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n141: - [ ] T019 [P] In `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md`, after the paragraph that starts `The ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides`, add one paragraph: A hub whose compiled route defers or asks to clarify, and whose modes have commands, renders as `<hub> (needs a mode)` with no command, for example `Advisor: live; use system-deep-loop (needs a mode) 0.82/0.20 pass.` The recommendation carries `needsMode: true`. A command on the same recommendation wins and the label is dropped. Verify: `rg -c \"needs a mode\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md` prints at least 2.\n142: \n143: ### Wave C4\n144: - [ ] T020 Floor check after wave C (`$SP/after-C.txt`). Run `FLOORCHECK C`. Verify: every gate in `FLOORCHECK` prints what it must, `RS` reports 0 failed against the wave B baseline with no recapture and `PLAIN` prints the same tune line as `$SP/after-B.txt`. When a gate fails, mark T021 `[B]` with that line and stop.\n145: \n146: ### Wave D1: deep pairs\n147: - [ ] T021 [P] Add one plain-wording anchor per deep mode (`EX`). In `PHRASE_BOOSTS`, right after the line `  'multi-ai-council': [['system-deep-loop', 1.2]],`, add the comment line `  // Plain wording for a deep run: each phrase asks for the effort a deep loop exists for.` and these three lines: `  'find out everything': [['system-deep-loop', 1]],`, `  'findings stop changing': [['system-deep-loop', 1.2], ['sk-code', -0.6]],` and `  'several models': [['system-deep-loop', 1]],`. Verify: `rg -c \"'find out everything'|'findings stop changing'|'several models'\" $EX` prints 3, `VEX` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n148: - [ ] T022 [P] Add one alias per deep mode to the deep-loop hub and refresh it (`HRL`, `$MS/system-deep-loop/manifest.json`, `$MA/system-deep-loop/manifest.json`). In `HRL` `vocabularyClasses`, append `\"find out everything\"` to the end of `research-aliases`, `\"findings stop changing\"` to the end of `review-aliases` and `\"several models\"` to the end of `ai-council-aliases`. Keep each array on its one line. Then run `REFRESH system-deep-loop`, `HUBOK system-deep-loop`, `RT system-deep-loop $CF_LOOP > $SP/routes-deep-loop-after.tsv` and `BINT`. Verify: `diff $SP/routes-deep-loop-before.tsv $SP/routes-deep-loop-after.tsv | rg '^>'` prints exactly `> cmd-deep-ai-council-plain-tune-01\t{\"action\":\"route\",\"t\":[\"ai-council\"]}`, `> cmd-deep-research-plain-tune-01\t{\"action\":\"route\",\"t\":[\"research\"]}` and `> cmd-deep-review-plain-tune-01\t{\"action\":\"route\",\"t\":[\"review\"]}`, `rg -n '^nm-system-deep-loop-01' $SP/routes-deep-loop-after.tsv` prints the same line as in the before file, `REFRESH` printed `0`, every `HUBOK` check prints what it must and `BINT` prints `ℹ fail 0`. When the diff lists any other id, revert `HRL`, run `REFRESH system-deep-loop` again and mark the task `[B]` with that id.\n149: \n150: ### Wave D2\n151: - [ ] T023 Rebuild and recapture after wave D (`BASE`). Run `BLD`, then `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain.tune' $BASE` prints `{\"own\":7,\"attached\":13,\"combined\":20,\"total\":45}` (expectation), `jq -r '.rows[\"cmd-deep-research-plain-tune-01\"].top, .attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-deep-research-plain-tune-01\"], .attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-deep-review-plain-tune-01\"], .attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-deep-ai-council-plain-tune-01\"]' $BASE` prints `system-deep-loop`, `/deep:research`, `/deep:review` and `/deep:ai-council`, `jq -r '.attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"nm-system-deep-loop-01\"] // \"none\"' $BASE` prints `none`, `jq -c '.metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached' $BASE` prints `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` twice and `RS` reports 0 failed.\n152: \n153: ### Wave D3\n154: - [ ] T024 Floor check after wave D (`$SP/after-D.txt`). Run `FLOORCHECK D`. Verify: every gate in `FLOORCHECK` prints what it must, and `diff <(rg '^(FLOOR|TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-C.txt) <(rg '^(FLOOR|TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-D.txt)` is run and its output, empty or not, is left for T025.\n155: \n156: ### Wave D4\n157: - [ ] T025 [C] Decide whether wave E runs. Wave E runs only when `diff <(rg '^(FLOOR|TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-C.txt) <(rg '^(FLOOR|TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-D.txt)` prints nothing (a rise counts as a change), `PY` exits 0, `PAR`, `RS`, `VDS` and `SUITE` report 0 failed and T023 printed the three deep commands. Then append `wave E: runs` to `$SP/after-D.txt`. Otherwise mark T026 to T029 `[B]` with the first line that differs, append `wave E: skipped, <line>` and go to T030. Verify: `rg -c '^wave E: (runs|skipped)' $SP/after-D.txt` prints 1.\n158: \n159: ### Wave E1: create anchors, only when T025 says runs\n160: - [ ] T026 Add one narrow anchor per wrong-top create row (`EX`). In `PHRASE_BOOSTS`, right after the line that starts `  'create testing playbook':`, add the comment line `  // Plain wording for three create modes whose rival skills share their words.` and these three lines: `  'prompt shortcut': [['sk-doc', 1]],`, `  'intent file': [['sk-doc', 1]],` and `  'test script package': [['create:manual-testing-playbook', 1.8], ['command-create-manual-testing-playbook', 1.2], ['sk-doc', 0.2]],`. Verify: `rg -c \"'prompt shortcut'|'intent file'|'test script package'\" $EX` prints 3, `VEX` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n161: \n162: ### Wave E2\n163: - [ ] T027 Rebuild and recapture after wave E (`BASE`). Run `BLD`, then `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain.tune' $BASE` prints `{\"own\":8,\"attached\":15,\"combined\":23,\"total\":45}` (expectation), `jq -r '.attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-create-command-plain-tune-01\"], .attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-create-goal-plain-tune-01\"], .rows[\"cmd-create-manual-testing-playbook-plain-tune-01\"].top1' $BASE` prints `/create:command`, `/create:goal` and `true`, `jq -c '.metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached' $BASE` prints `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` twice and `RS` reports 0 failed.\n164: \n165: ### Wave E3\n166: - [ ] T028 Floor check after wave E (`$SP/after-E.txt`). Run `FLOORCHECK E`. Verify: every gate in `FLOORCHECK` prints what it must, and `diff <(rg '^(FLOOR|TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-D.txt) <(rg '^(FLOOR|TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-E.txt)` prints nothing.\n167: \n168: ### Wave E4\n169: - [ ] T029 [C] Keep or revert wave E. When T028's diff prints a line, or any gate fails, revert `EX` to its wave D state, rerun T027's commands and write `wave E: reverted, <line>` into `$SP/after-E.txt`. Otherwise write `wave E: kept`. Verify: `rg -c '^wave E: (kept|reverted)' $SP/after-E.txt` prints 1.\n170: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n175: ## Phase 3: Verification\n176: \n177: - [ ] T030 Run every floor from the final state (`$SP/after-final.txt`). Run `FLOORCHECK final`, plus `VCA`, `VGT`, `VDHT`, `VFHT`, `VARC`, `VHFT`, `VSCH`, `VEX`, `DBC`, `HUBOK sk-doc` and `HUBOK system-deep-loop`. Verify: every gate prints what it must, `EVAL` is no lower than its line in `$SP/before.txt`, `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain.tune.combined' $BASE` prints a number above 9 and each command's line sits beside its before line in `after-final.txt`.\n178: - [ ] T031 Count the 16 plain chat rows that now carry their command (`$SP/chat-after.json`). Run `IDS='[\"cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-02\",\"cmd-create-benchmark-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-changelog-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-command-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-diff-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-feature-catalog-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-goal-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-manual-testing-playbook-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-deep-ai-council-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-deep-research-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-deep-review-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-doctor-agents-md-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-doctor-ci-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-doctor-deep-loop-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-doctor-helper-plain-tune-01\"]'` and `jq --argjson ids \"$IDS\" '.attachedMetrics.attachedRows as $a | [.rows | to_entries[] | select(.key as $k | $ids | index($k)) | {id: .key, top: .value.top, top1: .value.top1, top3: .value.top3, attached: ($a[.key] // null), carried: (.value.top1 or .value.top3 or (($a[.key] // null) == .value.expected_command))}]' $BASE > $SP/chat-after.json`. Verify: `jq 'length' $SP/chat-after.json` prints 16 and `jq '[.[] | select(.carried)] | length' $SP/chat-after.json` prints 16 when wave E was kept, or 13 when it was skipped or reverted. A lower count is recorded in `implementation-summary.md` with the row ids, not hidden.\n179: - [ ] T032 Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md .skilled/commands/create .skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/ROUTER.md \"$CF_DOC\" \"$CF_LOOP\"` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9])\"` prints nothing, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD --name-only | rg 'package(-lock)?\\.json$'` prints nothing and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled specs/sk-doc` lists only files named in this phase's tasks.\n180: - [ ] T033 [C] Read three live heads through the Claude prompt hook. Make one folder with `D=$(mktemp -d \"$TMPDIR/ssa-head-XXXXXX\")` and, for each prompt `use the sk-doc skill`, `use the system-deep-loop skill` and `confirm the AGENTS.md sections in this repo have not drifted`, pipe `{\"prompt\":<prompt>,\"cwd\":\"<repo root>\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"head-<n>\"}` into `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js` with `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR=\"$D\"`, and keep the first line of `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`. Then stop only a process whose command line names `$D` and remove `$D`. Verify: the first two lines contain `use sk-doc (needs a mode)` and `use system-deep-loop (needs a mode)`, the third contains `/doctor:agents-md check (run)` and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-head-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing. When the hook answers from the Python scorer (no `compiledRoute`, as seen on 2026-11-13), record the three lines and the cause as found, and mark this task `[B]` rather than passing it on the unit tests alone.\n181: - [ ] T034 Write `implementation-summary.md` with the per-wave plain tune line, the chat count, the wave E decision and every deviation, and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/012-plain-offer-reach --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n182: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: <!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n187: ## Completion Criteria\n188: \n189: - [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`, or wave E's four tasks `[B]` with the line T025 recorded\n190: - [ ] No other `[B]` task remaining\n191: - [ ] The committed baseline reads `attachedMetrics.plain.tune.combined` above 9 and every floor holds against `scratch/before.txt`\n192: <!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n193: \n194: ---\n195: \n196: <!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n197: ## Cross-References\n198: \n199: - **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n200: - **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n201: - **Acceptance criteria**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n202: - **Research**: `../009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/research-segment-2.md`\n203: - **Planning simulations**: `scratch/planning/`\n204: <!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n205: \n206: ---\n207: \n208: ## Verification Checklist\n209: \n210: <!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n211: ## Verification Protocol\n212: \n213: | Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n214: |----------|----------|-------------------|\n215: | **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n216: | **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n217: | **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n218: \n219: A blocked task gets `[B]` and one line naming what blocks it. A moved floor blocks the next wave.\n220: <!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n221: \n222: ---\n223: \n224: <!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n225: ## Pre-Implementation\n226: \n227: - [ ] CHK-001 [P0] The before numbers, the route tables and the plain row state are in `scratch/` (T001 to T003).\n228: - [ ] CHK-002 [P0] The coordinator confirmed phase 011 is committed before wave A1 started.\n229: <!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n230: \n231: ---\n232: \n233: <!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n234: ## Code Quality\n235: \n236: - [ ] CHK-010 [P0] `npm run typecheck` in `ADV` exits 0 after every wave (`FLOORCHECK`).\n237: - [ ] CHK-011 [P0] No code comment names a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id (T032).\n238: - [ ] CHK-012 [P0] The generated blocks in `command-attach.ts` are untouched and the deriver check prints `fresh` (T013, T030).\n239: - [ ] CHK-013 [P1] A ranked `ask` command and a hub outside `MODE_COMMANDS` keep today's behaviour (T005, T013, T016).\n240: <!-- /ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n241: \n242: ---\n243: \n244: <!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n245: ## Testing Checklist\n246: \n247: - [ ] CHK-020 [P0] Every floor holds after every wave (T009, T012, T020, T024, T028, T030).\n248: - [ ] CHK-021 [P0] No silence row and no canary prompt changes route after either hub edit (T010, T022).\n249: - [ ] CHK-022 [P0] Each hub serves fresh, its served and authored manifests match and the guard reports 0 failures (T010, T022, T030).\n250: - [ ] CHK-023 [P0] The parity test and the Python test pass (`FLOORCHECK`).\n251: - [ ] CHK-024 [P1] The chat rows carry their command in the baseline (T031), and the live heads show the label and the named doctor command (T033).\n252: <!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n253: \n254: ---\n255: \n256: <!-- ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n257: ## FIX ADDENDUM: FIX COMPLETENESS\n258: \n259: - [ ] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] The gap is class `cross-consumer`: the hand-off, two hub vocabularies, the phrase table and the brief each lose a different family of rows.\n260: - [ ] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Every reader of the new field is listed: `rg -n \"needsMode\" .skilled --glob '!**/node_modules/**' --glob '!**/dist/**'` names `command-attach.ts`, the schema, `subprocess.ts`, the handler, the fallback, the renderer and their tests.\n261: - [ ] CHK-FIX-003 [P1] Every reader of the inserted doctor id is listed: `rg -n \"DOCTOR_HELPER_BRIDGE_ID\" $ADV --glob '!dist/**'`.\n262: - [ ] CHK-FIX-004 [P1] Evidence names the commit it ran on.\n263: <!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n264: \n265: ---\n266: \n267: <!-- ANCHOR:security -->\n268: ## Security\n269: \n270: - [ ] CHK-030 [P0] No task reads or prints holdout prompt text, and `RT` filters `split` before it reads a prompt (T002).\n271: - [ ] CHK-031 [P0] No task writes to the home folder, installs anything or adds a dependency (T032).\n272: <!-- /ANCHOR:security -->\n273: \n274: ---\n275: \n276: <!-- ANCHOR:docs -->\n277: ## Documentation\n278: \n279: - [ ] CHK-040 [P1] `spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md` and `implementation-summary.md` state the same numbers.\n280: - [ ] CHK-041 [P1] The hook guide and the doctor feature catalog page match the code (T007, T019).\n281: <!-- /ANCHOR:docs -->\n282: \n283: ---\n284: \n285: <!-- ANCHOR:file-org -->\n286: ## File Organization\n287: \n288: - [ ] CHK-050 [P1] Route tables, floor checks, the chat count and the planning simulations live in `scratch/` only.\n289: <!-- /ANCHOR:file-org -->\n290: \n291: ---\n292: \n293: <!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n294: ## Verification Summary\n295: \n296: | Category | Total | Verified |\n297: |----------|-------|----------|\n298: | P0 Items | 12 | 0/12 |\n299: | P1 Items | 8 | 0/8 |\n300: | P2 Items | 0 | 0/0 |\n301: \n302: **Verification Date**: 2026-11-13\n303: <!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/012-plain-offer-reach/tasks.md, lines 1-303\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Tasks: Phase 12: plain-offer-reach\"\n3: description: \"Ordered worker-sized tasks in five waves: name the matched doctor command in the hand-off, add sk-doc paraphrase aliases, label a hub that needs a mode, pair each deep mode with a scorer anchor and a hub alias and, only when the floors stay put, anchor three create rows. Each wave ends with a recapture and a floor check.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"plain offer reach tasks\"\n6: importance_tier: \"normal\"\n7: contextType: \"general\"\n8: ---\n9: <!-- SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE: tasks-core | v2.4 -->\n10: # Tasks: Phase 12: plain-offer-reach\n11: \n12: <!-- SPECKIT_LEVEL: 2 -->\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: <!-- ANCHOR:notation -->\n17: ## Task Notation\n18: \n19: | Prefix | Meaning |\n20: |--------|---------|\n21: | `[ ]` | Pending |\n22: | `[x]` | Completed |\n23: | `[P]` | Parallelizable |\n24: | `[B]` | Blocked |\n25: | `[C]` | Coordinator task: a decision, a revert, a live hook run or an operator step. No worker runs it |\n26: \n27: **Task Format**: `T### [P?] Description (file path). Verify: <command> prints <expected output>`\n28: \n29: Every command runs from the repository root unless a task says otherwise.\n30: - `ADV` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime`. `TC` is `npm run typecheck` in `ADV`, which must exit 0. `BLD` is `npm run build` in `ADV`, which must exit 0. `SP` is this phase's `scratch/` folder.\n31: - `DH` is `$ADV/lib/scorer/doctor-helper-handoff.ts` and `DHT` its test `$ADV/tests/doctor-helper-handoff.vitest.ts`. `VDHT` is `npx vitest run tests/doctor-helper-handoff.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n32: - `FU` is `$ADV/lib/scorer/fusion.ts`. `FHT` is `$ADV/tests/scorer/doctor-handoff-command.vitest.ts` (new) and `VFHT` is `npx vitest run tests/scorer/doctor-handoff-command.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n33: - `DP` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/doctor-prompts.jsonl`. `VDS` is `npx vitest run tests/doctor-routing-slice.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n34: - `CA` is `$ADV/lib/command-attach.ts` and `CAT` its test `$ADV/tests/command-attach.vitest.ts`. `VCA` is `npx vitest run tests/command-attach.vitest.ts` in `ADV`. `DBC` is `node $ADV/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs --check | jq -r .status`, which must print `fresh`.\n35: - `SCH` is `$ADV/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts` and `VSCH` is `npx vitest run tests/schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.vitest.ts` in `ADV`. `SUB` is `$ADV/lib/subprocess.ts`.\n36: - `AR` is `$ADV/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts` and `ARC` its test `$ADV/tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts`. `VARC` is `npx vitest run tests/handlers/advisor-recommend-compiled-route-option.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n37: - `HFB` is `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts` and `HFT` its test `$ADV/tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-envelope.vitest.ts`. `VHFT` is `npx vitest run tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-envelope.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n38: - `RD` is `$ADV/lib/render.ts` and `GT` is `$ADV/tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts`. `VGT` is `npx vitest run tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts tests/command-head-length.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n39: - `EX` is `$ADV/lib/scorer/lanes/explicit.ts`. `VEX` is `npx vitest run tests/command-bridge-resolution-guard.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n40: - `HRD` is `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/hub-router.json` and `HRL` is `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/hub-router.json`. `CF_DOC` is `.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/009-parent-hub-rollout/007-sk-doc/fixtures/canary-cases.v1.json` and `CF_LOOP` is `.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/009-parent-hub-rollout/002-system-deep-loop/fixtures/canary-cases.v1.json`.\n41: - `MS` is `.skilled/bin/lib/compiled-routing/013-live-activation/activation` (the served manifests) and `MA` is `specs/sk-doc/z_archive/019-skill-routing-refactor/015-router-unification-program/013-live-activation/activation` (their authored twins).\n42: - `REFRESH <hub>` is three commands in order: `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-manifest.cjs refresh --hub <hub> --skill-root .skilled/skills/<hub>`, then `cp $MS/<hub>/manifest.json $MA/<hub>/manifest.json`, then `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-guard.cjs --json | jq .failures`, which must print `0`.\n43: - `HUBOK <hub>` is four checks: `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-manifest.cjs freshness --hub <hub> --skill-root .skilled/skills/<hub> | jq -r .causeCode` prints `fresh`, `cmp $MS/<hub>/manifest.json $MA/<hub>/manifest.json` prints nothing, `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-sync.cjs --check | tail -1` prints `all 55 served copies match their authored files` and `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs .skilled/skills/<hub> | tail -1` prints a line that starts with `OK: parent-skill-check`.\n44: - `BINT` is `node --test .skilled/bin/tests/compiled-route-*.test.cjs`, which must print `ℹ fail 0`.\n45: - `CAP` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs`. `BASE` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-routing-baseline.json`. `CORPUS` is `$ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-prompts.jsonl`. `RS` is `npx vitest run tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` in `ADV`.\n46: - `RT` is a shell function that prints the compiled route of every non-holdout corpus row and every canary prompt on one hub, as `<id><TAB>{\"action\":...,\"t\":[...]}`, and never prints a prompt. Define it in the shell first: `RT() { { jq -r 'select(.split!=\"holdout\") | [.id, .prompt] | @tsv' \"$CORPUS\"; jq -r '.cases[] | select(.prompt) | [\"canary:\" + .id, .prompt] | @tsv' \"$2\"; } | while IFS=$'\\t' read -r id p; do printf '%s\\t%s\\n' \"$id\" \"$(printf '%s' \"$p\" | node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub \"$1\" --prompt-stdin | jq -c '{action, t: [.targets[]? | .workflowMode]}')\"; done; }`. Call it as `RT sk-doc $CF_DOC` (154 lines) or `RT system-deep-loop $CF_LOOP` (139 lines).\n47: - `FLOOR` is `node $CAP | jq -c '.metrics.typed_recall_at_1, .metrics.false_offer'`, which must print at least `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and exactly `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`.\n48: - `TYPED` is `node $CAP | jq -c '.attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached, .attachedMetrics.typed'`, which must print exactly `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` and the typed line in `$SP/before.txt` (`{\"own\":24,\"attached\":20,\"combined\":44,\"total\":44}` once phase 011 is committed).\n49: - `PLAIN` is `node $CAP | jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain.tune, .attachedMetrics.plain.holdout'`. It is a measurement, not a gate. The holdout line is a count and never a prompt.\n50: - `EVAL` is `node $ADV/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs | jq -c '[.metrics.full_corpus_top1.correct,.metrics.holdout_top1.correct,.metrics.ambiguity_top1.correct,.metrics.gold_none_false_fire.value]'`, which must print numbers no lower than `[159,50,20]` and no lower than its line in `$SP/before.txt`, with the fourth no higher than 5.\n51: - `PY` is `python3 $ADV/tests/python/test_skill_advisor.py`, which must exit 0. `PAR` is `npx vitest run tests/parity/python-ts-parity.vitest.ts` in `ADV`, which must report 0 failed. `SUITE` is `npx vitest run` in `ADV`, whose `Test Files` and `Tests` summary lines must name no `failed` count.\n52: - `FLOORCHECK <X>` is: run `BLD`, then `FLOOR`, `TYPED`, `PLAIN`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `PY`, `PAR`, `RS`, `VDS`, `BINT`, `GUARD` (`node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-guard.cjs --json | jq .failures`) and `SUITE`, and write into `$SP/after-<X>.txt` one line per output line, each prefixed with the shorthand name and a colon, such as `FLOOR: {\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` or `TC: exit 0`. For the test runners write the summary line. It passes when every gate above prints what it must. `$SP/before.txt` uses the same format.\n53: \n54: Rules for every task:\n55: - Each task is one change to the files it names. A task that names a module and its test adds the behaviour and the cases that pin it.\n56: - No task reads or prints holdout prompt text. A script filters on `split` before it uses a prompt and prints row ids only. Tune and none rows may be read.\n57: - No code comment may name a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id. State the durable reason instead. T032 checks it.\n58: - No task writes to the home folder, installs anything, adds a dependency, edits `AGENTS.md`, edits a file under `.skilled/commands/create/`, edits a `ROUTER.md` or edits a canary fixture.\n59: - A task that edits a hub `hub-router.json` ends with `REFRESH <hub>` and `HUBOK <hub>` in the same task.\n60: - The brief head keeps the phase 005 form. The `(needs a mode)` label is the one addition, and `GT` is re-pinned in the same task as the renderer.\n61: - A `.md` edit routes through `sk-doc` and a code edit through `sk-code` before the first write. Every task re-reads its anchor text first, because phase 011 edited the same files.\n62: \n63: Worker waves. Tasks in one wave touch different files and can run in parallel. Each task owns the files in parentheses. A wave starts only after the previous wave's last task printed its expected output.\n64: - Wave 0: T001 (`$SP/before.txt`), T002 (`$SP/routes-*-before.tsv`), T003 (`$SP/plain-before.json`). Read-only for the repository.\n65: - Gate: no task from wave A1 on starts before the coordinator confirms that phase 011 is committed, because it edits `CA`, `FU`, `AR`, `SCH`, `CAP` and `BASE`.\n66: - Wave A1: T004 (`DH`, `DHT`).\n67: - Wave A2: T005 (`FU`, `FHT`).\n68: - Wave A3: T006 (`DP`), T007 (the doctor feature catalog page).\n69: - Wave A4: T008 (`BASE`). Wave A5: T009 (`$SP/after-A.txt`).\n70: - Wave B1: T010 (`HRD`, both `sk-doc` manifests). Wave B2: T011 (`BASE`). Wave B3: T012 (`$SP/after-B.txt`).\n71: - Wave C1: T013 (`CA`, `CAT`), T014 (`SCH`), T015 (`SUB`).\n72: - Wave C2: T016 (`AR`, `ARC`), T017 (`RD`, `GT`).\n73: - Wave C3: T018 (`HFB`, `HFT`), T019 (the hook guide). Wave C4: T020 (`$SP/after-C.txt`).\n74: - Wave D1: T021 (`EX`), T022 (`HRL`, both deep-loop manifests). Wave D2: T023 (`BASE`). Wave D3: T024 (`$SP/after-D.txt`). Wave D4: T025 [C].\n75: - Wave E1: T026 (`EX`). Wave E2: T027 (`BASE`). Wave E3: T028 (`$SP/after-E.txt`). Wave E4: T029 [C].\n76: - Verification, one at a time: T030, T031, T032, T033 [C], T034.\n77: \n78: Expected plain tune line (`.attachedMetrics.plain.tune`) per wave. Only the floors are a hard gate:\n79: \n80: | After | own | attached | combined |\n81: |-------|-----|----------|----------|\n82: | Wave 0 | 2 | 7 | 9 |\n83: | A | 7 | 7 | 14 |\n84: | B | 7 | 10 | 17 |\n85: | C | 7 | 10 | 17 |\n86: | D | 7 | 13 | 20 |\n87: | E | 8 | 15 | 23 |\n88: <!-- /ANCHOR:notation -->\n89: \n90: ---\n91: \n92: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n93: ## Phase 1: Setup\n94: \n95: - [x] T001 Record the before numbers (`$SP/before.txt`). Run `BLD` only if `node $CAP` refuses a stale build, then run `FLOOR`, `TYPED`, `PLAIN`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `PY`, `PAR`, `RS`, `VDS`, `VCA`, `VGT`, `VDHT`, `VARC`, `VHFT`, `VSCH`, `VEX`, `DBC`, `BINT`, `SUITE`, `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-guard.cjs --json | jq .failures`, `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route-sync.cjs --check | tail -1` and `HUBOK sk-doc` and `HUBOK system-deep-loop`, and write them into `before.txt` in the `FLOORCHECK` line format. Verify: `FLOOR` prints `{\"correct\":24,\"total\":44}` and `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}`, `TYPED` prints `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` and a typed line, `PLAIN` prints `{\"own\":2,\"attached\":7,\"combined\":9,\"total\":45}` first, `EVAL` prints numbers no lower than `[159,50,20]` with a fourth no higher than 5, `TC` and `PY` exit 0, the guard prints `0` and `wc -l < $SP/before.txt` prints at least 25. When a gate is red here, stop and mark the phase `[B]` with that line, because a red before number cannot be held. When `SUITE` names failed files, list them in `before.txt`, and from then on the `SUITE` gate is \"no failed file outside that list\".\n96: - [x] T002 Record every compiled route before any hub edit (`$SP/routes-sk-doc-before.tsv`, `$SP/routes-deep-loop-before.tsv`). Define `RT` and run `RT sk-doc $CF_DOC > $SP/routes-sk-doc-before.tsv` and `RT system-deep-loop $CF_LOOP > $SP/routes-deep-loop-before.tsv`. Verify: `wc -l < $SP/routes-sk-doc-before.tsv` prints 154, `wc -l < $SP/routes-deep-loop-before.tsv` prints 139, `rg -c 'holdout' $SP/routes-*-before.tsv` prints nothing or 0 for both and `rg -n '^nm-system-deep-loop-01' $SP/routes-deep-loop-before.tsv` prints `nm-system-deep-loop-01\t{\"action\":\"route\",\"t\":[\"research\"]}`.\n97: - [x] T003 Record each plain tune row's state (`$SP/plain-before.json`): run `node $CAP | jq '.attachedMetrics.attachedRows as $a | [.rows | to_entries[] | select(.key | test(\"plain-tune\")) | {id: .key, expected: .value.expected_command, top: .value.top, top1: .value.top1, top3: .value.top3, attached: ($a[.key] // null)}]' > $SP/plain-before.json`. Verify: `jq 'length' $SP/plain-before.json` prints 45, `jq '[.[] | select(.top1)] | length' $SP/plain-before.json` prints 2 and `jq -r '.[] | select(.id==\"cmd-doctor-ci-plain-tune-01\") | .top' $SP/plain-before.json` prints `command-doctor-helper`.\n98: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-1 -->\n99: \n100: ---\n101: \n102: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n103: ## Phase 2: Implementation\n104: \n105: ### Wave A1: the doctor hand-off module\n106: - [x] T004 Return the ranked runnable, add the gerund cue and add a repair request cue (`DH`, `DHT`). In `DH`: (1) in `interface HelperCandidate` add `readonly runnable?: string;` after `readonly command: string;`. (2) In `interface DoctorProblemMatch` add `readonly runnable: string;` after `readonly command: string;`, with the doc comment `` /** The command with its subcommand, such as `/doctor:agents-md check`. */ ``. (3) In `matchDoctorProblem`, change the returned object to `{ command: first.command, runnable: typeof first.runnable === 'string' && first.runnable !== '' ? first.runnable : first.command, score: first.score }`. (4) In `STRONG_PROBLEM_CUE`, add the line `  '\\\\bstopped converg\\\\w*\\\\b',` right after the line that starts `  '\\\\bnever (?:converg`. (5) After the `WEAK_PROBLEM_CUE` declaration add `const GENERIC_REPAIR_CUE = /\\b(?:repair path|i have a problem)\\b/i;` with the comment: a request for a repair route that names no problem the ranker knows still belongs to /doctor:helper, which lists the routes and their reasons, and the two phrases are exact so ordinary work requests never carry them. (6) After `doctorProblemCue` add `export function genericRepairCue(prompt: string): boolean { return GENERIC_REPAIR_CUE.test(prompt); }` with the doc comment `/** True when the prompt asks for a repair route without naming the problem. */`. (7) In the module header, replace `and if it does the advisor offers` and the line after it, `/doctor:helper, which then names the command.`, with `and if it does the advisor offers the command the ranker named, or` and `/doctor:helper when that command asks before it runs.`. In `DHT` add cases: `doctorProblemCue('my research loop stopped converging and I cannot see why')` is `'strong'`, `matchDoctorProblem('confirm the AGENTS.md sections in this repo have not drifted', WORKSPACE)?.runnable` is `'/doctor:agents-md check'`, `matchDoctorProblem('show me every GitHub Actions workflow and whether each is enabled', WORKSPACE)?.runn... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n107: \n108: ### Wave A2: the hand-off inserts the named command\n109: - [x] T005 Insert the ranked `run` command instead of the helper (`FU`, `FHT`). In `FU`: (1) add `COMMAND_ROWS` to the import from `'../command-attach.js'` and `genericRepairCue` to the import from `'./doctor-helper-handoff.js'`. (2) Right above the comment block that precedes `export function applyDoctorHelperHandoff(`, add `export function runnableDoctorBridgeId(runnable: string, projection: AdvisorProjection): string | undefined` with this body: for each `[id, row]` of `Object.entries(COMMAND_ROWS)`, skip it unless `id.startsWith(DOCTOR_BRIDGE_PREFIX)`, `row.command === runnable` and `row.policy === 'run'`, and return `id` when `projection.skills.some((skill) => skill.id === id)`. After the loop return `undefined`. Its comment says the ranker names a runnable such as `/doctor:agents-md check`, and this returns that bridge when it runs without asking and the projection holds it. (3) Replace the four comment lines above `applyDoctorHelperHandoff` with: `// A doctor problem described in other words than a bridge's own phrases reaches`, `// no doctor bridge. When none passes and none was typed, /doctor:helper's ranker`, `// decides whether the prompt is a doctor problem. If it is, the command it ranks`, `// first passes when that command runs without asking, and the helper passes`, `// otherwise. A plain request for a repair route with no ranked command and no`, `// passing skill also gets the helper.`. (4) In the function, replace `if (!match) return [...ranked];` with `if (!match && (skillPasses || !genericRepairCue(context.prompt))) return [...ranked];`, and prefix the next condition with `match && ` so it reads `if (match && cue === null && match.score < DOCTOR_HELPER_UNCUED_MIN_SCORE) return [...ranked];`. (5) Before `const existing =` add `const handoffId = (match ? runnableDoctorBridgeId(match.runnable, context.projection) : undefined) ?? DOCTOR_HELPER_BRIDGE_ID;` and `const handoffSkill = context.projection.skills.find((skill) => skill.id === handoffId) ?? he... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n110: \n111: ### Wave A3\n112: - [x] T006 [P] Relabel the three hand-off rows that now get a named command (`DP`). Change exactly three lines and nothing else. In the line with `\"id\":\"doctor-b-helper-handoff-01\"`, replace `\"expected_top\":\"command-doctor-helper\"` with `\"expected_top\":\"command-doctor-agents-md-check\"`. In the line with `\"id\":\"doctor-b-helper-handoff-02\"`, replace `\"expected_second\":\"command-doctor-helper\"` with `\"expected_second\":\"command-doctor-deep-loop\"`. In the line with `\"id\":\"doctor-b-helper-handoff-04\"`, replace `\"expected_top\":\"command-doctor-helper\"` with `\"expected_top\":\"command-doctor-setup\"`. Verify: `VDS` reports 0 failed, `rg -c '\"expected_top\":\"command-doctor-helper\"' $DP` prints 3, `rg -c '\"expected_second\":\"command-doctor-helper\"' $DP` prints 1 and `wc -l < $DP` prints 78.\n113: - [x] T007 [P] In `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md`, in the paragraph that starts `A doctor bridge passes on its own wording`, replace the text `` and if it does the `/doctor:helper` bridge passes so the operator is offered the command that names the right one. `` with the text `` and if it does the command the ranker puts first passes by name when its policy is `run`, and `/doctor:helper` passes instead when that command asks first or has no bridge. A prompt that asks for a `repair path` or says `I have a problem`, with no ranked command and no passing skill, gets `/doctor:helper`. `stopped converging` counts as a wrong state beside a passing skill. `` (both without the outer spaces). Verify: `rg -c \"repair path\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md` prints 1 and `rg -c \"is offered the command that names the right one\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/scorer-fusion/doctor-command-routing.md` prints nothing or 0.\n114: \n115: ### Wave A4\n116: - [x] T008 Rebuild and recapture after wave A (`BASE`). Run `BLD`, then `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain.tune' $BASE` prints `{\"own\":7,\"attached\":7,\"combined\":14,\"total\":45}` (expectation), `jq -r '.rows[\"cmd-doctor-agents-md-plain-tune-01\"].top, .rows[\"cmd-doctor-ci-plain-tune-01\"].top, .rows[\"cmd-doctor-helper-plain-tune-01\"].top, .rows[\"cmd-doctor-deep-loop-plain-tune-01\"].top3' $BASE` prints `command-doctor-agents-md-check`, `command-doctor-ci-report`, `command-doctor-helper` and `true`, `jq -c '.metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached' $BASE` prints `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` twice and `RS` reports 0 failed. When the tune line differs from the expectation but the floors hold, write the actual line into `$SP/after-A.txt` in T009 and continue.\n117: \n118: ### Wave A5\n119: - [x] T009 Floor check after wave A (`$SP/after-A.txt`). Run `FLOORCHECK A`. Verify: every gate in `FLOORCHECK` prints what it must, and `diff <(rg '^(TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/before.txt) <(rg '^(TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-A.txt)` prints nothing, because the simulation moved no typed or scorer eval row. When a gate fails or that diff prints a line, mark T010 `[B]` with that line and stop.\n120: \n121: ### Wave B1: sk-doc paraphrase aliases\n122: - [ ] T010 Add six task-shaped aliases to the `sk-doc` hub and refresh it (`HRD`, `$MS/sk-doc/manifest.json`, `$MA/sk-doc/manifest.json`). In `HRD` `vocabularyClasses`, append to the end of each `keywords` array: `create-agent-aliases` gets `\"agent definition\"` and `\"specialist persona file\"`, `create-diff-aliases` gets `\"before and after my rewrite\"`, `create-goal-aliases` gets `\"intent file\"` and `create-command-aliases` gets `\"prompt shortcut\"` and `\"confirm flag\"`. Change nothing else, and keep the file's two-space indentation. Then run `REFRESH sk-doc`, `HUBOK sk-doc`, `RT sk-doc $CF_DOC > $SP/routes-sk-doc-after.tsv` and `BINT`. Verify: `jq '[.vocabularyClasses[].keywords | length] | add' $HRD` prints 6 more than before the edit, `diff $SP/routes-sk-doc-before.tsv $SP/routes-sk-doc-after.tsv | rg '^>' | cut -f1 | tr '\\n' ' '` prints `> cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-01 > cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-02 > cmd-create-command-plain-tune-01 > cmd-create-diff-plain-tune-01 > cmd-create-goal-plain-tune-01 `, `rg -n 'sk-create-(agent|command|diff|goal)' $SP/routes-sk-doc-after.tsv | rg -c 'plain-tune'` prints at least 5, `REFRESH` printed `0`, every `HUBOK` check prints what it must and `BINT` prints `ℹ fail 0`. When the diff lists any other id, in particular an `mns-`, `nm-`, `sv-` or `canary:` id, revert `HRD`, run `REFRESH sk-doc` again and mark the task `[B]` with that id.\n123: \n124: ### Wave B2\n125: - [ ] T011 Rebuild and recapture after wave B (`BASE`). Run `BLD`, then `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain.tune' $BASE` prints `{\"own\":7,\"attached\":10,\"combined\":17,\"total\":45}` (expectation), `jq -r '.attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-01\"], .attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-02\"], .attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-create-diff-plain-tune-01\"]' $BASE` prints `/create:agent`, `/create:agent` and `/create:diff`, `jq -c '.metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached' $BASE` prints `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` twice and `RS` reports 0 failed.\n126: \n127: ### Wave B3\n128: - [ ] T012 Floor check after wave B (`$SP/after-B.txt`). Run `FLOORCHECK B`. Verify: every gate in `FLOORCHECK` prints what it must. When a gate fails, mark T013 `[B]` with that line and stop.\n129: \n130: ### Wave C1: the mode flag\n131: - [ ] T013 [P] Add `routeNeedsMode` (`CA`, `CAT`). At the end of `CA`, after `attachCommand`, add `export function routeNeedsMode(skillId: string, compiledRoute: unknown): boolean` whose body is: `const route = asRecord(compiledRoute);`, return false when `!route`, return false when `route.action !== 'defer' && route.action !== 'clarify'`, and return `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(MODE_COMMANDS, skillId)`. Its comment says a hub that withholds its command because the prompt names no single mode must still tell the reader why no command came with it, and only a hub whose modes map to commands withholds one. Do not touch the generated blocks. In `CAT` add cases: `routeNeedsMode('system-deep-loop', { action: 'defer' })` is true, `routeNeedsMode('sk-doc', { action: 'clarify' })` is true, `routeNeedsMode('sk-doc', { action: 'route', selectionKind: 'single', targets: [{ workflowMode: 'sk-create-readme' }] })` is false, `routeNeedsMode('sk-code', { action: 'defer' })` is false, `routeNeedsMode('sk-doc', undefined)` is false and `routeNeedsMode('sk-doc', ['defer'])` is false. Verify: `VCA` reports 0 failed, `TC` exits 0 and `DBC` prints `fresh`.\n132: - [ ] T014 [P] Add `needsMode` to the recommendation schema (`SCH`). In `AdvisorRecommendationSchema`, right after `ownerSkillId: z.string().min(1).optional(),`, add `needsMode: z.literal(true).optional(),` with the comment: set only when a hub held back its command because the prompt names no single mode, so the head can say so. Verify: `rg -c \"needsMode\" $SCH` prints 1, `VSCH` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n133: - [ ] T015 [P] Add `needsMode` to `AdvisorRecommendation` (`SUB`). In `export interface AdvisorRecommendation`, right after `readonly ownerSkillId?: string;`, add `readonly needsMode?: boolean;` with the comment: true when a hub held back its command because the prompt names no single mode. Verify: `rg -c \"readonly needsMode\" $SUB` prints 1 and `TC` exits 0.\n134: \n135: ### Wave C2\n136: - [ ] T016 [P] Set `needsMode` in the compiled route enrichment (`AR`, `ARC`). Add `routeNeedsMode` to the import from `'../lib/command-attach.js'`. In `enrichCompiledRoutes`, replace the last branch of the return, `: { ...recommendation, compiledRoute };`, with `: { ...recommendation, compiledRoute, ...(!command && routeNeedsMode(recommendation.skillId, compiledRoute) ? { needsMode: true as const } : {}) };`. In `ARC`, add `needsMode?: boolean` to the recommendation type of `ResponsePayload` and add one case: mock `execFileSync` to return `JSON.stringify({ action: 'defer', hubId: 'sk-doc', selectionKind: null, targets: [] })`, call `handleAdvisorRecommend` with prompt `Write a README for the ledger module` and `options: { includeCompiledRoute: true }`, and expect the `sk-doc` row to have `needsMode` true and no `command`. In the same case, mock a `defer` for `sk-code` and call it with prompt `Implement a TypeScript feature with focused tests for the ledger.`, and expect the `sk-code` row to have no `needsMode` key. Use prompts no other case in the file uses, because the handler caches by prompt. Verify: `VARC` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n137: - [ ] T017 [P] Print `(needs a mode)` and re-pin the golden heads (`RD`, `GT`). In `renderAdvisorBrief`, right after the `if (!topLabel) { ... }` block, add `` const headLabel = top.needsMode === true && top.command === undefined && commandRowFor(top.skill) === undefined ? `${topLabel} (needs a mode)` : topLabel; `` with the comment: a hub that withheld its command because the prompt names no single mode says so, so the reader asks for the mode instead of improvising one. In both head strings, the ambiguous one and the `use` one, replace `${topLabel}` with `${headLabel}`. Change nothing else in `RD`. In `GT`, add `readonly needsMode?: boolean;` to `RecommendationInput` and add two `HEAD_CASES`: name `a hub that needs a mode says so instead of naming a command` with `{ skill: 'system-deep-loop', needsMode: true, confidence: 0.82, uncertainty: 0.2 }` and `expectedHead` `Advisor: live; use system-deep-loop (needs a mode) 0.82/0.20 pass.`, and name `a command on the hub wins over the mode flag` with `{ skill: 'system-deep-loop', kind: 'command', command: '/deep:review', policy: 'ask', ownerSkillId: 'system-deep-loop', needsMode: true, confidence: 0.82, uncertainty: 0.16 }` and `expectedHead` `Advisor: live; use system-deep-loop → /deep:review (ask) 0.82/0.16 pass.`. Every existing case keeps its `expectedHead`. Verify: `VGT` reports 0 failed, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -- $GT | rg -c '^-.*expectedHead'` prints nothing or 0 and `TC` exits 0.\n138: \n139: ### Wave C3\n140: - [ ] T018 [P] Keep `needsMode` from the CLI (`HFB`, `HFT`). In `recommendationFromCli`, right after the spread that keeps `ownerSkillId`, add `...(value.needsMode === true ? { needsMode: true } : {}),` with the comment: a hub that held back its command for want of a mode keeps that signal, so the head can say why no command came with it. In `HFT` add one case in the shape of `renders a CLI row without command fields from the generated table`: `resultFromCliData` with `recommendations: [{ skillId: 'system-deep-loop', confidence: 0.89, uncertainty: 0.12, needsMode: true }]`, and expect the first line of `renderAdvisorBrief(result)` to contain `use system-deep-loop (needs a mode)`. Verify: `VHFT` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n141: - [ ] T019 [P] In `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md`, after the paragraph that starts `The ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides`, add one paragraph: A hub whose compiled route defers or asks to clarify, and whose modes have commands, renders as `<hub> (needs a mode)` with no command, for example `Advisor: live; use system-deep-loop (needs a mode) 0.82/0.20 pass.` The recommendation carries `needsMode: true`. A command on the same recommendation wins and the label is dropped. Verify: `rg -c \"needs a mode\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md` prints at least 2.\n142: \n143: ### Wave C4\n144: - [ ] T020 Floor check after wave C (`$SP/after-C.txt`). Run `FLOORCHECK C`. Verify: every gate in `FLOORCHECK` prints what it must, `RS` reports 0 failed against the wave B baseline with no recapture and `PLAIN` prints the same tune line as `$SP/after-B.txt`. When a gate fails, mark T021 `[B]` with that line and stop.\n145: \n146: ### Wave D1: deep pairs\n147: - [ ] T021 [P] Add one plain-wording anchor per deep mode (`EX`). In `PHRASE_BOOSTS`, right after the line `  'multi-ai-council': [['system-deep-loop', 1.2]],`, add the comment line `  // Plain wording for a deep run: each phrase asks for the effort a deep loop exists for.` and these three lines: `  'find out everything': [['system-deep-loop', 1]],`, `  'findings stop changing': [['system-deep-loop', 1.2], ['sk-code', -0.6]],` and `  'several models': [['system-deep-loop', 1]],`. Verify: `rg -c \"'find out everything'|'findings stop changing'|'several models'\" $EX` prints 3, `VEX` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n148: - [ ] T022 [P] Add one alias per deep mode to the deep-loop hub and refresh it (`HRL`, `$MS/system-deep-loop/manifest.json`, `$MA/system-deep-loop/manifest.json`). In `HRL` `vocabularyClasses`, append `\"find out everything\"` to the end of `research-aliases`, `\"findings stop changing\"` to the end of `review-aliases` and `\"several models\"` to the end of `ai-council-aliases`. Keep each array on its one line. Then run `REFRESH system-deep-loop`, `HUBOK system-deep-loop`, `RT system-deep-loop $CF_LOOP > $SP/routes-deep-loop-after.tsv` and `BINT`. Verify: `diff $SP/routes-deep-loop-before.tsv $SP/routes-deep-loop-after.tsv | rg '^>'` prints exactly `> cmd-deep-ai-council-plain-tune-01\t{\"action\":\"route\",\"t\":[\"ai-council\"]}`, `> cmd-deep-research-plain-tune-01\t{\"action\":\"route\",\"t\":[\"research\"]}` and `> cmd-deep-review-plain-tune-01\t{\"action\":\"route\",\"t\":[\"review\"]}`, `rg -n '^nm-system-deep-loop-01' $SP/routes-deep-loop-after.tsv` prints the same line as in the before file, `REFRESH` printed `0`, every `HUBOK` check prints what it must and `BINT` prints `ℹ fail 0`. When the diff lists any other id, revert `HRL`, run `REFRESH system-deep-loop` again and mark the task `[B]` with that id.\n149: \n150: ### Wave D2\n151: - [ ] T023 Rebuild and recapture after wave D (`BASE`). Run `BLD`, then `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain.tune' $BASE` prints `{\"own\":7,\"attached\":13,\"combined\":20,\"total\":45}` (expectation), `jq -r '.rows[\"cmd-deep-research-plain-tune-01\"].top, .attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-deep-research-plain-tune-01\"], .attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-deep-review-plain-tune-01\"], .attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-deep-ai-council-plain-tune-01\"]' $BASE` prints `system-deep-loop`, `/deep:research`, `/deep:review` and `/deep:ai-council`, `jq -r '.attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"nm-system-deep-loop-01\"] // \"none\"' $BASE` prints `none`, `jq -c '.metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached' $BASE` prints `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` twice and `RS` reports 0 failed.\n152: \n153: ### Wave D3\n154: - [ ] T024 Floor check after wave D (`$SP/after-D.txt`). Run `FLOORCHECK D`. Verify: every gate in `FLOORCHECK` prints what it must, and `diff <(rg '^(FLOOR|TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-C.txt) <(rg '^(FLOOR|TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-D.txt)` is run and its output, empty or not, is left for T025.\n155: \n156: ### Wave D4\n157: - [ ] T025 [C] Decide whether wave E runs. Wave E runs only when `diff <(rg '^(FLOOR|TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-C.txt) <(rg '^(FLOOR|TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-D.txt)` prints nothing (a rise counts as a change), `PY` exits 0, `PAR`, `RS`, `VDS` and `SUITE` report 0 failed and T023 printed the three deep commands. Then append `wave E: runs` to `$SP/after-D.txt`. Otherwise mark T026 to T029 `[B]` with the first line that differs, append `wave E: skipped, <line>` and go to T030. Verify: `rg -c '^wave E: (runs|skipped)' $SP/after-D.txt` prints 1.\n158: \n159: ### Wave E1: create anchors, only when T025 says runs\n160: - [ ] T026 Add one narrow anchor per wrong-top create row (`EX`). In `PHRASE_BOOSTS`, right after the line that starts `  'create testing playbook':`, add the comment line `  // Plain wording for three create modes whose rival skills share their words.` and these three lines: `  'prompt shortcut': [['sk-doc', 1]],`, `  'intent file': [['sk-doc', 1]],` and `  'test script package': [['create:manual-testing-playbook', 1.8], ['command-create-manual-testing-playbook', 1.2], ['sk-doc', 0.2]],`. Verify: `rg -c \"'prompt shortcut'|'intent file'|'test script package'\" $EX` prints 3, `VEX` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n161: \n162: ### Wave E2\n163: - [ ] T027 Rebuild and recapture after wave E (`BASE`). Run `BLD`, then `node $CAP --write`. Verify: `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain.tune' $BASE` prints `{\"own\":8,\"attached\":15,\"combined\":23,\"total\":45}` (expectation), `jq -r '.attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-create-command-plain-tune-01\"], .attachedMetrics.attachedRows[\"cmd-create-goal-plain-tune-01\"], .rows[\"cmd-create-manual-testing-playbook-plain-tune-01\"].top1' $BASE` prints `/create:command`, `/create:goal` and `true`, `jq -c '.metrics.false_offer, .attachedMetrics.false_offer_attached' $BASE` prints `{\"count\":0,\"total\":35}` twice and `RS` reports 0 failed.\n164: \n165: ### Wave E3\n166: - [ ] T028 Floor check after wave E (`$SP/after-E.txt`). Run `FLOORCHECK E`. Verify: every gate in `FLOORCHECK` prints what it must, and `diff <(rg '^(FLOOR|TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-D.txt) <(rg '^(FLOOR|TYPED|EVAL): ' $SP/after-E.txt)` prints nothing.\n167: \n168: ### Wave E4\n169: - [ ] T029 [C] Keep or revert wave E. When T028's diff prints a line, or any gate fails, revert `EX` to its wave D state, rerun T027's commands and write `wave E: reverted, <line>` into `$SP/after-E.txt`. Otherwise write `wave E: kept`. Verify: `rg -c '^wave E: (kept|reverted)' $SP/after-E.txt` prints 1.\n170: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n175: ## Phase 3: Verification\n176: \n177: - [ ] T030 Run every floor from the final state (`$SP/after-final.txt`). Run `FLOORCHECK final`, plus `VCA`, `VGT`, `VDHT`, `VFHT`, `VARC`, `VHFT`, `VSCH`, `VEX`, `DBC`, `HUBOK sk-doc` and `HUBOK system-deep-loop`. Verify: every gate prints what it must, `EVAL` is no lower than its line in `$SP/before.txt`, `jq -c '.attachedMetrics.plain.tune.combined' $BASE` prints a number above 9 and each command's line sits beside its before line in `after-final.txt`.\n178: - [ ] T031 Count the 16 plain chat rows that now carry their command (`$SP/chat-after.json`). Run `IDS='[\"cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-agent-plain-tune-02\",\"cmd-create-benchmark-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-changelog-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-command-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-diff-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-feature-catalog-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-goal-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-create-manual-testing-playbook-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-deep-ai-council-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-deep-research-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-deep-review-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-doctor-agents-md-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-doctor-ci-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-doctor-deep-loop-plain-tune-01\",\"cmd-doctor-helper-plain-tune-01\"]'` and `jq --argjson ids \"$IDS\" '.attachedMetrics.attachedRows as $a | [.rows | to_entries[] | select(.key as $k | $ids | index($k)) | {id: .key, top: .value.top, top1: .value.top1, top3: .value.top3, attached: ($a[.key] // null), carried: (.value.top1 or .value.top3 or (($a[.key] // null) == .value.expected_command))}]' $BASE > $SP/chat-after.json`. Verify: `jq 'length' $SP/chat-after.json` prints 16 and `jq '[.[] | select(.carried)] | length' $SP/chat-after.json` prints 16 when wave E was kept, or 13 when it was skipped or reverted. A lower count is recorded in `implementation-summary.md` with the row ids, not hidden.\n179: - [ ] T032 Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md .skilled/commands/create .skilled/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/ROUTER.md \"$CF_DOC\" \"$CF_LOOP\"` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9])\"` prints nothing, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD --name-only | rg 'package(-lock)?\\.json$'` prints nothing and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled specs/sk-doc` lists only files named in this phase's tasks.\n180: - [ ] T033 [C] Read three live heads through the Claude prompt hook. Make one folder with `D=$(mktemp -d \"$TMPDIR/ssa-head-XXXXXX\")` and, for each prompt `use the sk-doc skill`, `use the system-deep-loop skill` and `confirm the AGENTS.md sections in this repo have not drifted`, pipe `{\"prompt\":<prompt>,\"cwd\":\"<repo root>\",\"hook_event_name\":\"UserPromptSubmit\",\"session_id\":\"head-<n>\"}` into `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js` with `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR=\"$D\"`, and keep the first line of `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`. Then stop only a process whose command line names `$D` and remove `$D`. Verify: the first two lines contain `use sk-doc (needs a mode)` and `use system-deep-loop (needs a mode)`, the third contains `/doctor:agents-md check (run)` and `ls -d \"$TMPDIR\"/ssa-head-* 2>/dev/null` prints nothing. When the hook answers from the Python scorer (no `compiledRoute`, as seen on 2026-11-13), record the three lines and the cause as found, and mark this task `[B]` rather than passing it on the unit tests alone.\n181: - [ ] T034 Write `implementation-summary.md` with the per-wave plain tune line, the chat count, the wave E decision and every deviation, and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/012-plain-offer-reach --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n182: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: <!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n187: ## Completion Criteria\n188: \n189: - [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`, or wave E's four tasks `[B]` with the line T025 recorded\n190: - [ ] No other `[B]` task remaining\n191: - [ ] The committed baseline reads `attachedMetrics.plain.tune.combined` above 9 and every floor holds against `scratch/before.txt`\n192: <!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n193: \n194: ---\n195: \n196: <!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n197: ## Cross-References\n198: \n199: - **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n200: - **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n201: - **Acceptance criteria**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n202: - **Research**: `../009-ux-performance-cross-cli-research/research/research-segment-2.md`\n203: - **Planning simulations**: `scratch/planning/`\n204: <!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n205: \n206: ---\n207: \n208: ## Verification Checklist\n209: \n210: <!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n211: ## Verification Protocol\n212: \n213: | Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n214: |----------|----------|-------------------|\n215: | **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n216: | **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n217: | **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n218: \n219: A blocked task gets `[B]` and one line naming what blocks it. A moved floor blocks the next wave.\n220: <!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n221: \n222: ---\n223: \n224: <!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n225: ## Pre-Implementation\n226: \n227: - [ ] CHK-001 [P0] The before numbers, the route tables and the plain row state are in `scratch/` (T001 to T003).\n228: - [ ] CHK-002 [P0] The coordinator confirmed phase 011 is committed before wave A1 started.\n229: <!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n230: \n231: ---\n232: \n233: <!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n234: ## Code Quality\n235: \n236: - [ ] CHK-010 [P0] `npm run typecheck` in `ADV` exits 0 after every wave (`FLOORCHECK`).\n237: - [ ] CHK-011 [P0] No code comment names a spec path, a phase number, a task id, an ADR or a REQ id (T032).\n238: - [ ] CHK-012 [P0] The generated blocks in `command-attach.ts` are untouched and the deriver check prints `fresh` (T013, T030).\n239: - [ ] CHK-013 [P1] A ranked `ask` command and a hub outside `MODE_COMMANDS` keep today's behaviour (T005, T013, T016).\n240: <!-- /ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n241: \n242: ---\n243: \n244: <!-- ANCHOR:testing -->\n245: ## Testing Checklist\n246: \n247: - [ ] CHK-020 [P0] Every floor holds after every wave (T009, T012, T020, T024, T028, T030).\n248: - [ ] CHK-021 [P0] No silence row and no canary prompt changes route after either hub edit (T010, T022).\n249: - [ ] CHK-022 [P0] Each hub serves fresh, its served and authored manifests match and the guard reports 0 failures (T010, T022, T030).\n250: - [ ] CHK-023 [P0] The parity test and the Python test pass (`FLOORCHECK`).\n251: - [ ] CHK-024 [P1] The chat rows carry their command in the baseline (T031), and the live heads show the label and the named doctor command (T033).\n252: <!-- /ANCHOR:testing -->\n253: \n254: ---\n255: \n256: <!-- ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n257: ## FIX ADDENDUM: FIX COMPLETENESS\n258: \n259: - [ ] CHK-FIX-001 [P0] The gap is class `cross-consumer`: the hand-off, two hub vocabularies, the phrase table and the brief each lose a different family of rows.\n260: - [ ] CHK-FIX-002 [P0] Every reader of the new field is listed: `rg -n \"needsMode\" .skilled --glob '!**/node_modules/**' --glob '!**/dist/**'` names `command-attach.ts`, the schema, `subprocess.ts`, the handler, the fallback, the renderer and their tests.\n261: - [ ] CHK-FIX-003 [P1] Every reader of the inserted doctor id is listed: `rg -n \"DOCTOR_HELPER_BRIDGE_ID\" $ADV --glob '!dist/**'`.\n262: - [ ] CHK-FIX-004 [P1] Evidence names the commit it ran on.\n263: <!-- /ANCHOR:fix-completeness -->\n264: \n265: ---\n266: \n267: <!-- ANCHOR:security -->\n268: ## Security\n269: \n270: - [ ] CHK-030 [P0] No task reads or prints holdout prompt text, and `RT` filters `split` before it reads a prompt (T002).\n271: - [ ] CHK-031 [P0] No task writes to the home folder, installs anything or adds a dependency (T032).\n272: <!-- /ANCHOR:security -->\n273: \n274: ---\n275: \n276: <!-- ANCHOR:docs -->\n277: ## Documentation\n278: \n279: - [ ] CHK-040 [P1] `spec.md`, `plan.md`, `tasks.md` and `implementation-summary.md` state the same numbers.\n280: - [ ] CHK-041 [P1] The hook guide and the doctor feature catalog page match the code (T007, T019).\n281: <!-- /ANCHOR:docs -->\n282: \n283: ---\n284: \n285: <!-- ANCHOR:file-org -->\n286: ## File Organization\n287: \n288: - [ ] CHK-050 [P1] Route tables, floor checks, the chat count and the planning simulations live in `scratch/` only.\n289: <!-- /ANCHOR:file-org -->\n290: \n291: ---\n292: \n293: <!-- ANCHOR:summary -->\n294: ## Verification Summary\n295: \n296: | Category | Total | Verified |\n297: |----------|-------|----------|\n298: | P0 Items | 12 | 0/12 |\n299: | P1 Items | 8 | 0/8 |\n300: | P2 Items | 0 | 0/0 |\n301: \n302: **Verification Date**: 2026-11-13\n303: <!-- /ANCHOR:summary -->"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/012-plain-offer-reach/tasks.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794544718334,"end":1794544718942}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794544721392,"sessionID":"ses_e2ccd29cbffeOwNLR1Qo8Fv8oA","part":{"id":"prt_1d3335df0001hDsX1qKuzCokYc","sessionID":"ses_e2ccd29cbffeOwNLR1Qo8Fv8oA","messageID":"msg_1d3333af9001OlcB5AR433xviP","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"5af4d0ad8b27d6ebdac3ba875186fc8b11cfc8c9","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":17185,"output":104,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"read":384,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794544724339,"sessionID":"ses_e2ccd29cbffeOwNLR1Qo8Fv8oA","part":{"id":"prt_1d3336973001My5zt2Uy0nMuB0","sessionID":"ses_e2ccd29cbffeOwNLR1Qo8Fv8oA","messageID":"msg_1d3335df6001RM2SnLgI6Iebqf","type":"step-start","snapshot":"5edc9c628514569ae67f3f0aa19b9ec52ca9ae1a"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794544727199,"sessionID":"ses_e2ccd29cbffeOwNLR1Qo8Fv8oA","part":{"partID":"prt_1d33373a6001AbFMP9FkB5HVmq","sessionID":"ses_e2ccd29cbffeOwNLR1Qo8Fv8oA","messageID":"msg_1d3335df6001RM2SnLgI6Iebqf","type":"tool","id":"call_85b0b88ad6ca4757bb920a91","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794544726950,"end":1794544727199}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794544727203,"sessionID":"ses_e2ccd29cbffeOwNLR1Qo8Fv8oA","part":{"partID":"prt_1d333721300253DKQ8yrlJFQRw","sessionID":"ses_e2ccd29cbffeOwNLR1Qo8Fv8oA","messageID":"msg_1d3335df6001RM2SnLgI6Iebqf","type":"tool","id":"call_94de1de368d44b959c4953dc","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md, lines 1-250\n1: ---\n2: title: Skill Advisor Hook Reference\n3: description: Operator contract for the maintained Skill Advisor adapters across Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi, and the OpenCode plugin. Surfaces a prompt-safe routing brief on each user prompt; advisory only and fail-open.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"skill advisor hook reference\"\n6:   - \"native-first advisor hooks\"\n7:   - \"advisor hook runtime matrix\"\n8:   - \"opencode plugin bridge\"\n9:   - \"advisor hook smoke tests\"\n10: importance_tier: important\n11: contextType: implementation\n12: version: 3.6.7.0\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Skill Advisor Hook Reference\n16: \n17: ---\n18: \n19: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n20: \n21: The Skill Advisor concern surfaces prompt-safe routing context at the moment a user submits a prompt: it scores the prompt against the maintained skill graph and, when a match clears the confidence threshold, injects a brief recommending the matching skill(s). The brief is advisory: it never replaces explicit skill loading, never persists raw prompt text, and never blocks the prompt on an advisor failure.\n22: \n23: The maintained advisor implementation lives in `system-skill-advisor/runtime/`. The four editor runtimes (Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin) each carry a thin `user-prompt-submit` shim in `system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/<runtime>/` that resolves and spawns the compiled advisor in `system-skill-advisor`. The real brief-building, scoring, freshness, and rendering logic lives in the advisor package. Pi carries a native TypeScript extension that imports the same advisor module in-process (Pi awaits input handlers before agent processing, so the old two-process blocking-spawn bridge stalled every send). OpenCode integrates through the committed plugin at `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js`, which shells out to the advisor CLI.\n24: \n25: The single most important property is that it **fails open**: the prompt always proceeds. When the advisor call times out, fails or finds no route, the handler and the OpenCode plugin emit the directives fallback instead of a brief. An unparseable payload, an unhandled error or a shim that cannot relay its child resolves to `{}`. Every adapter honors the `skill-advisor` kill-switch, default-on.\n26: \n27: Paths beginning with `runtime/` in the package-local tables resolve from `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/` (for the shims) or `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/` (for the advisor). Paths beginning with `.skilled/` are repository-root relative.\n28: \n29: ---\n30: \n31: ## 2. WHAT IT DOES\n32: \n33: On each user-prompt event the adapter:\n34: \n35: 1. **Kill-switch.** Checks `isHookEnabled('skill-advisor')` (master `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED` or the `skill-advisor` family). Disabled → empty/skipped prompt-safe output.\n36: 2. **Read the prompt + workspace.** Resolves the workspace root (install-anchored walk, not CWD-relative, so the hook stays correct off-root), reads the prompt text (bounded), and resolves the session id.\n37: 3. **Prompt gate.** `skippedAdvisorResultFor` uses `shouldFireAdvisor` to decline skip commands, short acknowledgements and prompts below its length threshold. A declined prompt returns a skipped result and does not spawn the CLI.\n38: 4. **CLI front door.** The hook calls `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli`, which runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` within the `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` budget (2,500 ms by default). It writes the request to the CLI's stdin, so the prompt never appears in the process table. The request carries at most the prompt's first 10,000 characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts, so a longer prompt is routed on its head instead of failing open. The Claude shim sets the child budget to 2,200 ms when the operator has not set the variable. An operator value above about 2,200 ms lets the shim kill the hook at 2,500 ms first, so the turn gets `{}`. The CLI owns the warm-daemon probe, starts the daemon when the socket is cold and falls back to the local Python scorer with a `degraded` result. The request carries `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. It also carries `compiledRouteTopOnly: true`, because each route lookup is a subprocess inside the hook's budget and the brief leads with the top pick. The hook keeps the `command` field and reads nothing from the route itself. A daemon still running an older build rejects these options with JSON-RPC `-32602`, and the CLI retries once without them.\n39: 5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. With `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1`, a surfaced `start` or `ask` command, or a `run` command that is not read-only, renders as `(parent-named only)`. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n40: 6. **Directive-lifecycle dedup.** `decideDirectiveLifecycleDelivery` suppresses a re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence, so a repeated prompt does not re-inject an identical brief. A repeat in a known session keeps the first line, a route or a fallback status, and drops the directives block.\n41: 7. **Emit.** Native adapters emit a `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON envelope. OpenCode appends to `output.system` via `experimental.chat.system.transform`. Pi sends a model-visible message.\n42: \n43: The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the routed skill or, when the recommendation resolves to a command, the skill that owns it and the runnable command. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n44: \n45: ### Brief head form\n46: \n47: A recommendation that resolves to a command renders one label, shared by the `use` and `ambiguous` heads:\n48: \n49: ```text\n50: <ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)\n51: ```\n52: \n53: The recommendation's own `ownerSkillId`, `command` and `policy` are authoritative, and the generated command row fills only what they omit. The policy parenthesis is dropped when no policy is known. A recommendation with no command row keeps its skill label rather than a command label.\n54: \n55: The `use` head reads `Advisor: <freshness>; use <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` For example:\n56: \n57: ```text\n58: Advisor: live; use system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start) 0.89/0.12 pass.\n59: ```\n60: \n61: The ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides: `Advisor: <freshness>; ambiguous: <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> vs <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` Each label part passes `sanitizeSkillLabel`, so a control character, a line break or an instruction-shaped label renders nothing rather than reaching the model. The `Also:` line still names a doctor command by its runnable string.\n62: \n63: ### Degraded contract\n64: \n65: When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI runs the local Python scorer and returns `status: ok` with `degraded: true` and `source: local-scorer`. The local scorer returns command row ids, which a reader cannot type, so the CLI rewrites each row before the brief renders:\n66: \n67: - A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n68: - Every other row collapses to its owner skill: `skillId` is `ownerSkillId` and `kind` is `skill`. It is dropped when that skill is already offered, so the degraded brief never repeats a skill.\n69: \n70: Rows below the threshold are discarded before either rule applies. The degraded brief therefore keeps the same head form and names a skill that exists rather than a raw row id.\n71: \n72: ---\n73: \n74: ## 3. PER-RUNTIME DELIVERY\n75: \n76: Every native runtime and Pi run the same hook handler, which gates the prompt, calls the advisor CLI and renders with `renderAdvisorBrief`. The OpenCode plugin calls the CLI itself. The native adapters differ in prompt event, transport and delivery channel.\n77: \n78: | Runtime | Adapter | Event / wiring | Payload difference it handles | Delivery |\n79: |---|---|---|---|---|\n80: | **Claude** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `system-skill-advisor/.../dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.claude/settings.json`, matcher `\"\"`, timeout 3s | Shim resolves the advisor target by walking up to the `.opencode` ancestor (install-anchored, not CWD-relative). Bounded stdin (1 MiB), bounded child stdio, 2.5s child timeout, `SIGKILL` on timeout | `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON on stdout. `{}` on any failure |\n81: | **Codex** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.codex/hooks.json`, timeout 3s | Same shim dispatch. `cd \"${CODEX_PROJECT_DIR:-$PWD}\"`. Fallback `printf` envelope on resolution failure | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n82: | **Cursor** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/cursor/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/cursor/user-prompt-submit.js` | `beforeSubmitPrompt` in `.cursor/hooks.json`, timeout 10s | Same shim dispatch | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n83: | **Devin** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.devin/hooks.v1.json`, matcher `\"\"`, timeout 10s | Same shim dispatch. `cd \"${DEVIN_PROJECT_DIR:-$PWD}\"`. Fallback `printf` envelope | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n84: | **Pi** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/pi/prompt-advisor.ts` (real file, re-exported by its one-line `.pi/extensions/` entry) | `user_input` / prompt event via `.pi/extensions/` | Imports the advisor module **in-process** (not a subprocess). Pi awaits input handlers before agent processing, so the old blocking-spawn bridge stalled every send. In-process calls remove the stall and let the module-level prompt cache work. Captures raw user text (bounded 32 KiB, 64-session LRU). The in-process call passes `runtime: 'pi'` and races a timer of the advisor budget plus 300 ms, delivering the fallback directives if the call hangs | Model-visible message. Delivery-state machine suppresses same-session re-delivery |\n85: | **OpenCode** | `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js` | Plugin, loaded by OpenCode's flat glob over `.opencode/plugins/`. `experimental.chat.system.transform` per prompt, `event` for session lifecycle, `tool` for status | Shells out to the advisor CLI (bounded timeout, stdout cap 256 KiB, grace termination), TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup, transform-dedup integration | Brief appended to `output.system`. `spec_kit_skill_advisor_status` tool returns a key=value report (no raw path leakage) |\n86: | **DSH** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/dsh/prompt-advisor.mjs`, imported by `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/prompt-advisor.mjs` | `agent/pre-step` through the `repo-guards` plugin, on unless the `promptAdvisor` row flag is `false` | Proxies the latest operator prompt through the compiled Claude advisor chain under the runtime label `dsh`. A fan-out leaf, marked by the lineage patch, gets no brief | Brief added to the step |\n87: \n88: OpenCode has no source-hook adapter in `system-spec-kit`. Its prompt-time integration is the committed plugin, which shells out to the advisor CLI and inherits the shared render contract. The hooks-tree index at `.skilled/hooks/skill-advisor/` mirrors the per-runtime shims and the OpenCode plugin via relative symlinks. This README is the symlink target.\n89: \n90: ### Starting a surfaced command\n91: \n92: The document behind a surfaced command is `.skilled/commands/<group>/<name>.md`. The model reads that document in full and follows it, on every runtime. It never tells the user to type a slash command, and it asks the user to type one only when it cannot read the file.\n93: \n94: | Runtime | Model can start a command itself | Basis |\n95: |---|---|---|\n96: | Claude Code | Yes, through the Skill tool, as a shortcut for the names it lists: 33 of the 45 names, including one `create` entry and none of the other twelve `create:*` names | The Skill tool lists names, not files. `create/skill.md` collides with the `SKILL.md` convention on a case-insensitive disk, so the folder reads as one skill |\n97: | Codex | No | The mirror is a pointer stub that tells the model to read the command file. There is no `.codex/skills` |\n98: | Cursor | No model path found, and the brief is dormant | The mirror is a pointer stub. Public Cursor docs were not fetched |\n99: | Devin | No command surface at all | Skills are exposed, commands are not |\n100: | Pi | No model path found | The mirror is a pointer stub. Public Pi docs were not fetched, so this is unverified beyond the repository |\n101: | OpenCode | No documented model path. A parent process can start one with `opencode run --command` | Commands are user-triggered templates, while skills are loaded by the model through a skill tool |\n102: | DSH | No | dsh has no agent-file loader and no command loader |\n103: \n104: ---\n105: \n106: ## 4. DIRECTORY TREE\n107: \n108: ```text\n109: system-skill-advisor/hooks/\n110: +-- skill-advisor-hook.md            # this reference (symlinked from .skilled/hooks/skill-advisor/README.md)\n111: +-- skill-advisor-hook-validation.md # validation/audit detail\n112: +-- claude/\n113: |   +-- README.md\n114: |   +-- directive-lifecycle-boundary.ts\n115: |   `-- user-prompt-submit.ts         # real advisor handler (Claude shape)\n116: +-- dsh/\n117: |   +-- README.md\n118: |   `-- prompt-advisor.mjs            # real DSH adapter (.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/ imports it)\n119: +-- pi/\n120: |   +-- README.md\n121: |   `-- prompt-advisor.ts             # real Pi extension (.pi/extensions/ re-exports it)\n122: `-- lib/\n123:     +-- README.md\n124:     +-- directive-lifecycle.ts, directive-lifecycle-contract.ts, directive-lifecycle-file-store.ts\n125:     +-- directive-lifecycle-store.py, directive-lifecycle-vectors.json\n126:     `-- skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts\n127: \n128: system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.{ts,js}  # thin shims (claude/codex/cursor/devin)\n129: system-skill-advisor/runtime/\n130: +-- dist/hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.js   # compiled advisor the shims spawn\n131: +-- lib/skill-advisor-brief.ts, render.ts, metrics.ts, subprocess.ts\n132: `-- scripts/skill_advisor.py                      # standalone Python advisor CLI\n133: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js         # OpenCode plugin\n134: ```\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. KEY FILES\n139: \n140: | File | Responsibility |\n141: |---|---|\n142: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` | The real advisor handler (Claude shape). Builds the brief, runs the CLI fallback, renders, applies directive-lifecycle dedup, emits the `additionalContext` envelope, persists a diagnostic record. The codex/cursor/devin siblings share the same advisor core over their runtime's payload shape. |\n143: | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.ts` | Thin process-boundary shims. Resolve the advisor target by walking up to the `.opencode` ancestor, spawn the compiled advisor with bounded stdin/stdio/timeout, and relay its stdout (or `{}` on any failure). |\n144: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/pi/prompt-advisor.ts` | Native Pi extension. Imports the advisor module in-process, captures raw user text (bounded LRU), runs a delivery-state machine that suppresses same-session re-delivery, and sends the brief as a model-visible message. Races the advisor call against a deadline of the advisor budget plus 300 ms and delivers the fallback directives if it hangs. |\n145: | `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js` | OpenCode plugin. `experimental.chat.system.transform` spawns the bridge and appends the brief to `output.system`. `event` manages session lifecycle and cache invalidation. `tool: spec_kit_skill_advisor_status` returns a sanitized status report. TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup and transform-dedup integration. |\n146: | `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs` | The CLI front door the OpenCode plugin spawns. Receives the `advisor_recommend` command line, returns the JSON payload on stdout. |\n147: | `system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/skill-advisor-brief.ts` | The brief builder: scoring, freshness, status, confidence/uncertainty. |\n148: | `system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts` | `renderAdvisorBrief` / `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`, produces the model-visible text. |\n149: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/directive-lifecycle.ts` | Directive-lifecycle dedup: decides whether a directive should be re-delivered to a session within its cadence. |\n150: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts` | `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli` is the hook's CLI caller. It runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` with the hook timeout and sends the request on stdin. The CLI owns daemon startup and local-scorer fallback. The function resolves the CLI, IPC bridge and database paths from one root. It searches `.skilled` first and `.opencode` second. |\n151: | `system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` | Standalone Python advisor CLI. The advisor CLI runs it as the local scorer when the daemon is unreachable. The hook never calls it directly. |\n152: \n153: ---\n154: \n155: ## 6. CONFIGURATION\n156: \n157: The advisor is enabled by default. Truthy disable values are `1`, `true`, `yes`, and `on` (case-insensitive) for the shared resolver.\n158: \n159: | Variable | Effect |\n160: |---|---|\n161: | `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED=1` | Canonical kill-switch. Every adapter checks `isHookEnabled('skill-advisor')` and returns empty/skipped prompt-safe output. |\n162: | `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` | Alias. Disables the native adapters. |\n163: | `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED=1` | Alias. Disables the OpenCode plugin. |\n164: | `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` | Legacy alias for the native adapters. The standalone Python CLI (`skill_advisor.py`) reads only this name, set to exactly `1`, from `runtime/schemas/compat-contract.json`. |\n165: | `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED=1` | Legacy alias for the OpenCode plugin. |\n166: | `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED=1` | Master switch that disables this concern along with every other repo hook. |\n167: \n168: Additional controls: `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_FORCE_LOCAL=1` (force local Python fallback where supported) and `--force-native` / `--force-local` / `--threshold` / `--stdin` (Python shim flags, default threshold `0.8`). `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` (default 3000) is owned by the `system-skill-advisor` hub: its live consumers are `runtime/lib/subprocess.ts`, `skill-advisor-brief.ts`, the OpenCode bridge and `skill_advisor.py`. On timeout the OpenCode bridge serves prompt-safe stale context with a timeout marker. OpenCode tuning: `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CACHE_TTL_MS` (default 5 min), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_TOKENS` (default 80), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_BRIEF_CHARS` (default 2 KiB), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_PROMPT_BYTES` (default 64 KiB, measured on the whole stdin request after JSON escaping), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_COMPILED_ROUTE_BOUNDING` (compiled-route summary bounding).\n169: \n170: Set a flag inline for one command, export it for a session, or persist it in `.skilled/hooks/hook-flags.env` (copied from `hook-flags.env.example`, gitignored). The environment always wins over the file, so a persisted default can be overridden for a single session.\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: ## 7. BOUNDARIES AND FLOW\n175: \n176: | Boundary | Rule |\n177: |---|---|\n178: | Advisory only | Hooks surface prompt-safe routing context. They do not replace explicit skill loading, persist raw prompt text, or block the user prompt on advisor failures. |\n179: | Fail-open | All adapters let the prompt proceed. A failed or empty advisor call yields the directives fallback. A parsing failure or unhandled error yields `{}`. The shims return `{}` on `TARGET_UNRESOLVED`, `CHILD_TIMEOUT`, `NONZERO_EXIT`, `EMPTY_OUTPUT`, `INVALID_JSON`, `INPUT_OVERFLOW`, and `SPAWN_ERROR`. |\n180: | Freshness | Freshness states `live`, `stale`, `absent`, `unavailable`. Status values `ok`, `skipped`, `degraded`, `fail_open`. Default confidence/uncertainty `0.8 / 0.35` unless overridden. |\n181: | Privacy | Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution. The OpenCode status tool sanitizes paths (`[configured-node]`, `[skill-advisor-bridge]`). |\n182: | Dedup | Directive-lifecycle dedup suppresses re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence. OpenCode adds transform-dedup across co-resident transforms. The plugin decides same-message transform dedup on the full advisor block before lifecycle reduction, so a repeated transform for the same message is suppressed. |\n183: | Diagnostics | Each hook record carries `runtime` (the Codex, Cursor and Devin adapters set `SPECKIT_RUNTIME` and Pi passes `pi`) plus `emittedBytes` and `directivesSuppressed`. Records are written only with `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` set, and the subprocess hook waits up to 100 ms for pending writes before exit. |\n184: | Bounded | Shims bound stdin (1 MiB), child stdio (1 MiB), child timeout (2.5s), and `SIGKILL` on timeout. OpenCode bounds bridge stdout (256 KiB) and uses grace termination. Pi bounds captured user text (32 KiB, 64-session LRU). |\n185: | Output | Native adapters emit one `additionalContext` JSON on stdout. OpenCode appends to `output.system`. Pi sends a model-visible message. No adapter writes to the TUI prompt line. |\n186: | Imports | Shims import Node builtins only and spawn the advisor by absolute path. The advisor core imports its `runtime/lib/*` siblings and the shared `hook-flags` resolver. Nothing outside the repo. |\n187: \n188: ### Operator freshness states\n189: \n190: | State | Meaning | Operator Action |\n191: | --- | --- | --- |\n192: | `live` | Current graph generation is trusted. | No action. |\n193: | `stale` | Sources are newer than graph state. | Use scored recommendations with a caveat, then rebuild. |\n194: | `absent` | Required graph state is missing. | Rebuild. Empty recommendations are expected until repaired. |\n195: | `unavailable` | Status cannot be read. | Inspect daemon logs and use a supported fallback. |\n196: \n197: ---\n198: \n199: ## 8. VALIDATION\n200: \n201: Build both maintained packages:\n202: \n203: ```bash\n204: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime run build\n205: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build\n206: ```\n207: \n208: Expected result: both builds succeed and `dist/hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.js` are produced.\n209: \n210: ```bash\n211: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime run typecheck\n212: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime test -- --reporter=default\n213: node --test .opencode/plugins/tests/system-skill-advisor.test.cjs\n214: ```\n215: \n216: Expected result: no type errors. All advisor package tests pass. The OpenCode plugin test passes.\n217: \n218: Native smoke tests (each targets an existing compiled file):\n219: \n220: ```bash\n221: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"update documentation with DQI checks\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\"}' | \\\n222:   node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js\n223: ```\n224: \n225: Expected: `{}` or `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` beginning with `Advisor:`. Repeat for the `codex`, `cursor`, and `devin` siblings.\n226: \n227: OpenCode plugin CLI smoke test:\n228: \n229: ```bash\n230: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"save this conversation context to memory\",\"options\":{\"topK\":3,\"includeAttribution\":false,\"includeAbstainReasons\":true,\"confidenceThreshold\":0.8,\"uncertaintyThreshold\":0.35}}' \\\n231:   | node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --format json\n232: ```\n233: \n234: Installation drift check (from a linked worktree with the required worktree flag):\n235: \n236: ```bash\n237: node .skilled/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs --check --allow-worktree\n238: ```\n239: \n240: This compares the repository's maintained Codex registration and adapter paths. A report about the user-global installation is workstation state, not a repository defect.\n241: \n242: ---\n243: \n244: ## 9. RELATED\n245: \n246: - [`skill-advisor-hook-validation.md`](skill-advisor-hook-validation.md): validation and audit detail for this concern.\n247: - [`claude/README.md`](claude/README.md), [`pi/README.md`](pi/README.md), [`lib/README.md`](lib/README.md): per-surface detail.\n248: - [`../../system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/README.md`](../../system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/README.md): the owning skill's hook contract (where the shims live).\n249: - [`../../../hooks/README.md`](../../../hooks/README.md): the unified hooks tree with the kill-switch index and coverage matrix.\n250: - [`../../../../.opencode/plugins/README.md`](../../../../.opencode/plugins/README.md): the OpenCode plugins folder that loads `system-skill-advisor.js`.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md, lines 1-250\n1: ---\n2: title: Skill Advisor Hook Reference\n3: description: Operator contract for the maintained Skill Advisor adapters across Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Pi, and the OpenCode plugin. Surfaces a prompt-safe routing brief on each user prompt; advisory only and fail-open.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"skill advisor hook reference\"\n6:   - \"native-first advisor hooks\"\n7:   - \"advisor hook runtime matrix\"\n8:   - \"opencode plugin bridge\"\n9:   - \"advisor hook smoke tests\"\n10: importance_tier: important\n11: contextType: implementation\n12: version: 3.6.7.0\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Skill Advisor Hook Reference\n16: \n17: ---\n18: \n19: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n20: \n21: The Skill Advisor concern surfaces prompt-safe routing context at the moment a user submits a prompt: it scores the prompt against the maintained skill graph and, when a match clears the confidence threshold, injects a brief recommending the matching skill(s). The brief is advisory: it never replaces explicit skill loading, never persists raw prompt text, and never blocks the prompt on an advisor failure.\n22: \n23: The maintained advisor implementation lives in `system-skill-advisor/runtime/`. The four editor runtimes (Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin) each carry a thin `user-prompt-submit` shim in `system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/<runtime>/` that resolves and spawns the compiled advisor in `system-skill-advisor`. The real brief-building, scoring, freshness, and rendering logic lives in the advisor package. Pi carries a native TypeScript extension that imports the same advisor module in-process (Pi awaits input handlers before agent processing, so the old two-process blocking-spawn bridge stalled every send). OpenCode integrates through the committed plugin at `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js`, which shells out to the advisor CLI.\n24: \n25: The single most important property is that it **fails open**: the prompt always proceeds. When the advisor call times out, fails or finds no route, the handler and the OpenCode plugin emit the directives fallback instead of a brief. An unparseable payload, an unhandled error or a shim that cannot relay its child resolves to `{}`. Every adapter honors the `skill-advisor` kill-switch, default-on.\n26: \n27: Paths beginning with `runtime/` in the package-local tables resolve from `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/` (for the shims) or `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/` (for the advisor). Paths beginning with `.skilled/` are repository-root relative.\n28: \n29: ---\n30: \n31: ## 2. WHAT IT DOES\n32: \n33: On each user-prompt event the adapter:\n34: \n35: 1. **Kill-switch.** Checks `isHookEnabled('skill-advisor')` (master `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED` or the `skill-advisor` family). Disabled → empty/skipped prompt-safe output.\n36: 2. **Read the prompt + workspace.** Resolves the workspace root (install-anchored walk, not CWD-relative, so the hook stays correct off-root), reads the prompt text (bounded), and resolves the session id.\n37: 3. **Prompt gate.** `skippedAdvisorResultFor` uses `shouldFireAdvisor` to decline skip commands, short acknowledgements and prompts below its length threshold. A declined prompt returns a skipped result and does not spawn the CLI.\n38: 4. **CLI front door.** The hook calls `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli`, which runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` within the `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` budget (2,500 ms by default). It writes the request to the CLI's stdin, so the prompt never appears in the process table. The request carries at most the prompt's first 10,000 characters, the most `advisor_recommend` accepts, so a longer prompt is routed on its head instead of failing open. The Claude shim sets the child budget to 2,200 ms when the operator has not set the variable. An operator value above about 2,200 ms lets the shim kill the hook at 2,500 ms first, so the turn gets `{}`. The CLI owns the warm-daemon probe, starts the daemon when the socket is cold and falls back to the local Python scorer with a `degraded` result. The request carries `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. It also carries `compiledRouteTopOnly: true`, because each route lookup is a subprocess inside the hook's budget and the brief leads with the top pick. The hook keeps the `command` field and reads nothing from the route itself. A daemon still running an older build rejects these options with JSON-RPC `-32602`, and the CLI retries once without them.\n39: 5. **Render.** `renderAdvisorBrief` produces the model-visible `Advisor:` text. When there is no brief, the handler emits `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`: one status line, then the directives block. The line reads `Advisor: outage (<status>)` with the command to route by hand when the advisor failed or timed out, `Advisor: no skill matched.` when it answered with nothing above threshold, and `Advisor: prompt skipped.` when the prompt gate declined. The `/doctor:setup` hint appears on no-match and outage lines for prompts that use set up, set-up or setup wording, install or reinstall wording including installation and installer, configure or configuration wording or `check` followed within three words by environment, env, config, hooks, tools, tooling, toolchain or skilled. The no-match line ends with ` For setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup.` The outage line places `for setup or health checks, run /doctor:setup;` before the hand-route command so it stays last. With `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1`, a surfaced `start` or `ask` command, or a `run` command that is not read-only, renders as `(parent-named only)`. Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution.\n40: 6. **Directive-lifecycle dedup.** `decideDirectiveLifecycleDelivery` suppresses a re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence, so a repeated prompt does not re-inject an identical brief. A repeat in a known session keeps the first line, a route or a fallback status, and drops the directives block.\n41: 7. **Emit.** Native adapters emit a `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON envelope. OpenCode appends to `output.system` via `experimental.chat.system.transform`. Pi sends a model-visible message.\n42: \n43: The injected brief begins with `Advisor:` and names the routed skill or, when the recommendation resolves to a command, the skill that owns it and the runnable command. A doctor command appears as its runnable `/doctor:` string on the top line or under a skill as an `Also:` second line. The handler returns `{}` when the hook is disabled, input cannot be parsed or an unhandled exception occurs. A shim also returns `{}` when it cannot resolve or relay valid child output, including on timeout. When the handler has no brief, it emits the directives fallback.\n44: \n45: ### Brief head form\n46: \n47: A recommendation that resolves to a command renders one label, shared by the `use` and `ambiguous` heads:\n48: \n49: ```text\n50: <ownerSkillId or skill> → <command> (<policy>)\n51: ```\n52: \n53: The recommendation's own `ownerSkillId`, `command` and `policy` are authoritative, and the generated command row fills only what they omit. The policy parenthesis is dropped when no policy is known. A recommendation with no command row keeps its skill label rather than a command label.\n54: \n55: The `use` head reads `Advisor: <freshness>; use <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` For example:\n56: \n57: ```text\n58: Advisor: live; use system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start) 0.89/0.12 pass.\n59: ```\n60: \n61: The ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides: `Advisor: <freshness>; ambiguous: <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> vs <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` Each label part passes `sanitizeSkillLabel`, so a control character, a line break or an instruction-shaped label renders nothing rather than reaching the model. The `Also:` line still names a doctor command by its runnable string.\n62: \n63: ### Degraded contract\n64: \n65: When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI runs the local Python scorer and returns `status: ok` with `degraded: true` and `source: local-scorer`. The local scorer returns command row ids, which a reader cannot type, so the CLI rewrites each row before the brief renders:\n66: \n67: - A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n68: - Every other row collapses to its owner skill: `skillId` is `ownerSkillId` and `kind` is `skill`. It is dropped when that skill is already offered, so the degraded brief never repeats a skill.\n69: \n70: Rows below the threshold are discarded before either rule applies. The degraded brief therefore keeps the same head form and names a skill that exists rather than a raw row id.\n71: \n72: ---\n73: \n74: ## 3. PER-RUNTIME DELIVERY\n75: \n76: Every native runtime and Pi run the same hook handler, which gates the prompt, calls the advisor CLI and renders with `renderAdvisorBrief`. The OpenCode plugin calls the CLI itself. The native adapters differ in prompt event, transport and delivery channel.\n77: \n78: | Runtime | Adapter | Event / wiring | Payload difference it handles | Delivery |\n79: |---|---|---|---|---|\n80: | **Claude** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `system-skill-advisor/.../dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.claude/settings.json`, matcher `\"\"`, timeout 3s | Shim resolves the advisor target by walking up to the `.opencode` ancestor (install-anchored, not CWD-relative). Bounded stdin (1 MiB), bounded child stdio, 2.5s child timeout, `SIGKILL` on timeout | `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON on stdout. `{}` on any failure |\n81: | **Codex** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.codex/hooks.json`, timeout 3s | Same shim dispatch. `cd \"${CODEX_PROJECT_DIR:-$PWD}\"`. Fallback `printf` envelope on resolution failure | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n82: | **Cursor** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/cursor/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/cursor/user-prompt-submit.js` | `beforeSubmitPrompt` in `.cursor/hooks.json`, timeout 10s | Same shim dispatch | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n83: | **Devin** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.devin/hooks.v1.json`, matcher `\"\"`, timeout 10s | Same shim dispatch. `cd \"${DEVIN_PROJECT_DIR:-$PWD}\"`. Fallback `printf` envelope | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n84: | **Pi** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/pi/prompt-advisor.ts` (real file, re-exported by its one-line `.pi/extensions/` entry) | `user_input` / prompt event via `.pi/extensions/` | Imports the advisor module **in-process** (not a subprocess). Pi awaits input handlers before agent processing, so the old blocking-spawn bridge stalled every send. In-process calls remove the stall and let the module-level prompt cache work. Captures raw user text (bounded 32 KiB, 64-session LRU). The in-process call passes `runtime: 'pi'` and races a timer of the advisor budget plus 300 ms, delivering the fallback directives if the call hangs | Model-visible message. Delivery-state machine suppresses same-session re-delivery |\n85: | **OpenCode** | `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js` | Plugin, loaded by OpenCode's flat glob over `.opencode/plugins/`. `experimental.chat.system.transform` per prompt, `event` for session lifecycle, `tool` for status | Shells out to the advisor CLI (bounded timeout, stdout cap 256 KiB, grace termination), TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup, transform-dedup integration | Brief appended to `output.system`. `spec_kit_skill_advisor_status` tool returns a key=value report (no raw path leakage) |\n86: | **DSH** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/dsh/prompt-advisor.mjs`, imported by `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/prompt-advisor.mjs` | `agent/pre-step` through the `repo-guards` plugin, on unless the `promptAdvisor` row flag is `false` | Proxies the latest operator prompt through the compiled Claude advisor chain under the runtime label `dsh`. A fan-out leaf, marked by the lineage patch, gets no brief | Brief added to the step |\n87: \n88: OpenCode has no source-hook adapter in `system-spec-kit`. Its prompt-time integration is the committed plugin, which shells out to the advisor CLI and inherits the shared render contract. The hooks-tree index at `.skilled/hooks/skill-advisor/` mirrors the per-runtime shims and the OpenCode plugin via relative symlinks. This README is the symlink target.\n89: \n90: ### Starting a surfaced command\n91: \n92: The document behind a surfaced command is `.skilled/commands/<group>/<name>.md`. The model reads that document in full and follows it, on every runtime. It never tells the user to type a slash command, and it asks the user to type one only when it cannot read the file.\n93: \n94: | Runtime | Model can start a command itself | Basis |\n95: |---|---|---|\n96: | Claude Code | Yes, through the Skill tool, as a shortcut for the names it lists: 33 of the 45 names, including one `create` entry and none of the other twelve `create:*` names | The Skill tool lists names, not files. `create/skill.md` collides with the `SKILL.md` convention on a case-insensitive disk, so the folder reads as one skill |\n97: | Codex | No | The mirror is a pointer stub that tells the model to read the command file. There is no `.codex/skills` |\n98: | Cursor | No model path found, and the brief is dormant | The mirror is a pointer stub. Public Cursor docs were not fetched |\n99: | Devin | No command surface at all | Skills are exposed, commands are not |\n100: | Pi | No model path found | The mirror is a pointer stub. Public Pi docs were not fetched, so this is unverified beyond the repository |\n101: | OpenCode | No documented model path. A parent process can start one with `opencode run --command` | Commands are user-triggered templates, while skills are loaded by the model through a skill tool |\n102: | DSH | No | dsh has no agent-file loader and no command loader |\n103: \n104: ---\n105: \n106: ## 4. DIRECTORY TREE\n107: \n108: ```text\n109: system-skill-advisor/hooks/\n110: +-- skill-advisor-hook.md            # this reference (symlinked from .skilled/hooks/skill-advisor/README.md)\n111: +-- skill-advisor-hook-validation.md # validation/audit detail\n112: +-- claude/\n113: |   +-- README.md\n114: |   +-- directive-lifecycle-boundary.ts\n115: |   `-- user-prompt-submit.ts         # real advisor handler (Claude shape)\n116: +-- dsh/\n117: |   +-- README.md\n118: |   `-- prompt-advisor.mjs            # real DSH adapter (.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/ imports it)\n119: +-- pi/\n120: |   +-- README.md\n121: |   `-- prompt-advisor.ts             # real Pi extension (.pi/extensions/ re-exports it)\n122: `-- lib/\n123:     +-- README.md\n124:     +-- directive-lifecycle.ts, directive-lifecycle-contract.ts, directive-lifecycle-file-store.ts\n125:     +-- directive-lifecycle-store.py, directive-lifecycle-vectors.json\n126:     `-- skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts\n127: \n128: system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.{ts,js}  # thin shims (claude/codex/cursor/devin)\n129: system-skill-advisor/runtime/\n130: +-- dist/hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.js   # compiled advisor the shims spawn\n131: +-- lib/skill-advisor-brief.ts, render.ts, metrics.ts, subprocess.ts\n132: `-- scripts/skill_advisor.py                      # standalone Python advisor CLI\n133: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js         # OpenCode plugin\n134: ```\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. KEY FILES\n139: \n140: | File | Responsibility |\n141: |---|---|\n142: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` | The real advisor handler (Claude shape). Builds the brief, runs the CLI fallback, renders, applies directive-lifecycle dedup, emits the `additionalContext` envelope, persists a diagnostic record. The codex/cursor/devin siblings share the same advisor core over their runtime's payload shape. |\n143: | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.ts` | Thin process-boundary shims. Resolve the advisor target by walking up to the `.opencode` ancestor, spawn the compiled advisor with bounded stdin/stdio/timeout, and relay its stdout (or `{}` on any failure). |\n144: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/pi/prompt-advisor.ts` | Native Pi extension. Imports the advisor module in-process, captures raw user text (bounded LRU), runs a delivery-state machine that suppresses same-session re-delivery, and sends the brief as a model-visible message. Races the advisor call against a deadline of the advisor budget plus 300 ms and delivers the fallback directives if it hangs. |\n145: | `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js` | OpenCode plugin. `experimental.chat.system.transform` spawns the bridge and appends the brief to `output.system`. `event` manages session lifecycle and cache invalidation. `tool: spec_kit_skill_advisor_status` returns a sanitized status report. TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup and transform-dedup integration. |\n146: | `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs` | The CLI front door the OpenCode plugin spawns. Receives the `advisor_recommend` command line, returns the JSON payload on stdout. |\n147: | `system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/skill-advisor-brief.ts` | The brief builder: scoring, freshness, status, confidence/uncertainty. |\n148: | `system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts` | `renderAdvisorBrief` / `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`, produces the model-visible text. |\n149: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/directive-lifecycle.ts` | Directive-lifecycle dedup: decides whether a directive should be re-delivered to a session within its cadence. |\n150: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts` | `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli` is the hook's CLI caller. It runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` with the hook timeout and sends the request on stdin. The CLI owns daemon startup and local-scorer fallback. The function resolves the CLI, IPC bridge and database paths from one root. It searches `.skilled` first and `.opencode` second. |\n151: | `system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` | Standalone Python advisor CLI. The advisor CLI runs it as the local scorer when the daemon is unreachable. The hook never calls it directly. |\n152: \n153: ---\n154: \n155: ## 6. CONFIGURATION\n156: \n157: The advisor is enabled by default. Truthy disable values are `1`, `true`, `yes`, and `on` (case-insensitive) for the shared resolver.\n158: \n159: | Variable | Effect |\n160: |---|---|\n161: | `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED=1` | Canonical kill-switch. Every adapter checks `isHookEnabled('skill-advisor')` and returns empty/skipped prompt-safe output. |\n162: | `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` | Alias. Disables the native adapters. |\n163: | `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED=1` | Alias. Disables the OpenCode plugin. |\n164: | `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` | Legacy alias for the native adapters. The standalone Python CLI (`skill_advisor.py`) reads only this name, set to exactly `1`, from `runtime/schemas/compat-contract.json`. |\n165: | `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED=1` | Legacy alias for the OpenCode plugin. |\n166: | `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED=1` | Master switch that disables this concern along with every other repo hook. |\n167: \n168: Additional controls: `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_FORCE_LOCAL=1` (force local Python fallback where supported) and `--force-native` / `--force-local` / `--threshold` / `--stdin` (Python shim flags, default threshold `0.8`). `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` (default 3000) is owned by the `system-skill-advisor` hub: its live consumers are `runtime/lib/subprocess.ts`, `skill-advisor-brief.ts`, the OpenCode bridge and `skill_advisor.py`. On timeout the OpenCode bridge serves prompt-safe stale context with a timeout marker. OpenCode tuning: `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_CACHE_TTL_MS` (default 5 min), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_TOKENS` (default 80), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_BRIEF_CHARS` (default 2 KiB), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_MAX_PROMPT_BYTES` (default 64 KiB, measured on the whole stdin request after JSON escaping), `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_COMPILED_ROUTE_BOUNDING` (compiled-route summary bounding).\n169: \n170: Set a flag inline for one command, export it for a session, or persist it in `.skilled/hooks/hook-flags.env` (copied from `hook-flags.env.example`, gitignored). The environment always wins over the file, so a persisted default can be overridden for a single session.\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: ## 7. BOUNDARIES AND FLOW\n175: \n176: | Boundary | Rule |\n177: |---|---|\n178: | Advisory only | Hooks surface prompt-safe routing context. They do not replace explicit skill loading, persist raw prompt text, or block the user prompt on advisor failures. |\n179: | Fail-open | All adapters let the prompt proceed. A failed or empty advisor call yields the directives fallback. A parsing failure or unhandled error yields `{}`. The shims return `{}` on `TARGET_UNRESOLVED`, `CHILD_TIMEOUT`, `NONZERO_EXIT`, `EMPTY_OUTPUT`, `INVALID_JSON`, `INPUT_OVERFLOW`, and `SPAWN_ERROR`. |\n180: | Freshness | Freshness states `live`, `stale`, `absent`, `unavailable`. Status values `ok`, `skipped`, `degraded`, `fail_open`. Default confidence/uncertainty `0.8 / 0.35` unless overridden. |\n181: | Privacy | Raw prompt text is never persisted in diagnostics, cache metadata, status, or attribution. The OpenCode status tool sanitizes paths (`[configured-node]`, `[skill-advisor-bridge]`). |\n182: | Dedup | Directive-lifecycle dedup suppresses re-delivery of the same directive to the same session within its cadence. OpenCode adds transform-dedup across co-resident transforms. The plugin decides same-message transform dedup on the full advisor block before lifecycle reduction, so a repeated transform for the same message is suppressed. |\n183: | Diagnostics | Each hook record carries `runtime` (the Codex, Cursor and Devin adapters set `SPECKIT_RUNTIME` and Pi passes `pi`) plus `emittedBytes` and `directivesSuppressed`. Records are written only with `SKILL_ADVISOR_DEBUG` set, and the subprocess hook waits up to 100 ms for pending writes before exit. |\n184: | Bounded | Shims bound stdin (1 MiB), child stdio (1 MiB), child timeout (2.5s), and `SIGKILL` on timeout. OpenCode bounds bridge stdout (256 KiB) and uses grace termination. Pi bounds captured user text (32 KiB, 64-session LRU). |\n185: | Output | Native adapters emit one `additionalContext` JSON on stdout. OpenCode appends to `output.system`. Pi sends a model-visible message. No adapter writes to the TUI prompt line. |\n186: | Imports | Shims import Node builtins only and spawn the advisor by absolute path. The advisor core imports its `runtime/lib/*` siblings and the shared `hook-flags` resolver. Nothing outside the repo. |\n187: \n188: ### Operator freshness states\n189: \n190: | State | Meaning | Operator Action |\n191: | --- | --- | --- |\n192: | `live` | Current graph generation is trusted. | No action. |\n193: | `stale` | Sources are newer than graph state. | Use scored recommendations with a caveat, then rebuild. |\n194: | `absent` | Required graph state is missing. | Rebuild. Empty recommendations are expected until repaired. |\n195: | `unavailable` | Status cannot be read. | Inspect daemon logs and use a supported fallback. |\n196: \n197: ---\n198: \n199: ## 8. VALIDATION\n200: \n201: Build both maintained packages:\n202: \n203: ```bash\n204: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime run build\n205: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build\n206: ```\n207: \n208: Expected result: both builds succeed and `dist/hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.js` are produced.\n209: \n210: ```bash\n211: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime run typecheck\n212: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime test -- --reporter=default\n213: node --test .opencode/plugins/tests/system-skill-advisor.test.cjs\n214: ```\n215: \n216: Expected result: no type errors. All advisor package tests pass. The OpenCode plugin test passes.\n217: \n218: Native smoke tests (each targets an existing compiled file):\n219: \n220: ```bash\n221: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"update documentation with DQI checks\",\"cwd\":\"'\"$PWD\"'\"}' | \\\n222:   node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js\n223: ```\n224: \n225: Expected: `{}` or `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` beginning with `Advisor:`. Repeat for the `codex`, `cursor`, and `devin` siblings.\n226: \n227: OpenCode plugin CLI smoke test:\n228: \n229: ```bash\n230: printf '%s' '{\"prompt\":\"save this conversation context to memory\",\"options\":{\"topK\":3,\"includeAttribution\":false,\"includeAbstainReasons\":true,\"confidenceThreshold\":0.8,\"uncertaintyThreshold\":0.35}}' \\\n231:   | node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --format json\n232: ```\n233: \n234: Installation drift check (from a linked worktree with the required worktree flag):\n235: \n236: ```bash\n237: node .skilled/bin/install-codex-hooks.mjs --check --allow-worktree\n238: ```\n239: \n240: This compares the repository's maintained Codex registration and adapter paths. A report about the user-global installation is workstation state, not a repository defect.\n241: \n242: ---\n243: \n244: ## 9. RELATED\n245: \n246: - [`skill-advisor-hook-validation.md`](skill-advisor-hook-validation.md): validation and audit detail for this concern.\n247: - [`claude/README.md`](claude/README.md), [`pi/README.md`](pi/README.md), [`lib/README.md`](lib/README.md): per-surface detail.\n248: - [`../../system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/README.md`](../../system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/README.md): the owning skill's hook contract (where the shims live).\n249: - [`../../../hooks/README.md`](../../../hooks/README.md): the unified hooks tree with the kill-switch index and coverage matrix.\n250: - [`../../../../.opencode/plugins/README.md`](../../../../.opencode/plugins/README.md): the OpenCode plugins folder that loads `system-skill-advisor.js`."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794544726548,"end":1794544727203}}}}
